window.__hvBdRegister("seneca-essays", {"key":"seneca-essays","sections":[{"i":0,"j":9,"loc":"On Anger, Book I (1 of 4)","title":"Anger described, defined, and denied to beasts","summary":"After a note on the collection and on Agrippina, Seneca answers Novatus's request to write on soothing anger. He paints anger as the most hideous passion, a short madness whose bodily signs match those of the insane, and lists the ruin it has brought on cities, princes and nations; the text then breaks off and resumes with crowds angry at gladiators and boys striking the ground. Defending a definition of anger as a desire to punish, he replies to objections and compares Aristotle's view. Beasts, lacking reason, have only impulses resembling anger, and anger differs from irascibility as drunkenness from being a drunkard.","ideas":["Anger is a brief madness, visible in face and body","Anger is born of injury, real or intended, and seeks punishment","Only reasoning beings can be angry; animals have mere semblances of passion","Anger (a state) is distinct from irascibility (a disposition)"],"terms":[{"t":"ira (anger)","d":"For Seneca, a passion consisting in the desire to punish one thought to have done injury."},{"t":"iracundia (irascibility)","d":"The settled tendency to anger, as distinct from a single fit of it."}]},{"i":10,"j":18,"loc":"On Anger, Book I (2 of 4)","title":"Anger is neither natural nor useful","summary":"Seneca asks whether anger accords with nature and argues that man, born for mutual help and love, is the opposite of anger, which is born for mutual ruin. Correction is sometimes needed, but should proceed like a physician's graded treatment, without anger; he adds Plato's argument that a good man does no hurt. Against those who would keep a moderated anger as a spur to courage, he argues that passions are easier to exclude than to rule, since once admitted they overpower reason and the mind is itself turned into passion. Answering Aristotle, he holds that a passion that obeys reason is no longer anger, and that in war anger ruins barbarians while Fabius and the Scipios won by deliberation.","ideas":["Human nature is social and gentle; anger seeks ruin","Punishment should heal, as a physician escalates remedies","Passion must be stopped at the frontier, since once inside it cannot be limited","A moderate passion is only a moderate evil; reason needs no help from vice"],"terms":[{"t":"ratio (reason)","d":"The ruling power of the mind, which Seneca says is strong only while it stays apart from the passions."},{"t":"Fabius","d":"The Roman general whose delaying tactics against Hannibal Seneca offers as victory over anger."}]},{"i":19,"j":23,"loc":"On Anger, Book I (3 of 4)","title":"Duty without anger: punishing as healing","summary":"A good man whose father is murdered will avenge or protect him from duty, not anger; Seneca rebukes Theophrastus for appealing to common feeling. He argues that a good thing grows better as it increases, but anger grows worse, so it is no good; it replaces courage rather than aiding it. Since wrongdoing comes from error, the wise man should treat sinners as a father or physician, removing the incurable without hatred, as Socrates refused to strike a slave while angry. Punishment is graded like medicine from reproof to death, the magistrate should imitate the law, which defines offences without rage, and the wise man feels only the scar or semblance of passion, as Zeno said.","ideas":["Filial piety suffices as a motive without anger","Hating the erring would mean hating oneself, since all err","Graded penalties, even death, can be applied as cures","The wise man feels hints of passion but not passion itself"],"terms":[{"t":"Theophrastus","d":"Aristotle's successor, quoted as holding that good men must be angry at wrongs to friends and at bad men."},{"t":"Zeno's scar","d":"Zeno's saying that a scar remains in the wise man's mind after the wound has healed."}]},{"i":24,"j":30,"loc":"On Anger, Book I (4 of 4)","title":"Anger as a bad judge and false greatness","summary":"Against Aristotle's claim that passions can serve as arms, Seneca argues that they cannot be taken up and laid down at will, while reason is a weapon that obeys its owner. Anger is inconsistent, quick to cool, and unequal in punishment; it will not hear evidence, as shown by Gnaeus Piso, who executed three innocent men when the supposedly murdered soldier reappeared. The calm judge weighs intention and looks to the future, punishing, as Plato says, so that no one sins again. Book I ends by denying that anger is magnanimity: sayings like Sulla's era motto about hatred and fear, and Gaius Caesar's challenge to Jupiter, are monstrosity, and nothing is great that is not tranquil.","ideas":["Reason perseveres; anger rushes and then flags","The Piso episode: anger invents crimes where none exist","Just punishment weighs motive and looks forward, not back","Greatness rests on goodness; anger is only swollen vanity"],"terms":[{"t":"magnitudo animi (greatness of mind)","d":"A firm, sound and uniform disposition that Seneca says cannot exist without goodness."},{"t":"Gnaeus Piso","d":"A Roman commander whose angry sentence on three soldiers illustrates anger's injustice."}]},{"i":31,"j":35,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (1 of 6)","title":"Anger requires the mind's assent","summary":"Book II asks whether anger arises from choice or from involuntary impulse. Seneca states the Stoic view that anger needs the mind's approval, since it combines the judgment that one has been wronged with the judgment that one should take revenge. Involuntary reactions, such as shivering, blushing, a thrill at stage scenes, or a soldier starting at a trumpet, are only rudiments of passion; anger is the later rush that treats the impression as true and acts on it. He sets out three movements: an involuntary first stir, a second joined to a judgment, and a third that overrides reason. Gratuitous cruelty, as in Phalaris, Hannibal and Volesus, is not anger but ferocity grown from long indulgence.","ideas":["The first shock of an apparent injury is not yet anger","Anger is a voluntary fault and so can be put to flight by reason","Passions grow through three stages, the last beyond control","Cruelty for pleasure is a disease beyond anger"],"terms":[{"t":"assent","d":"The mind's approval of an impression, which Seneca holds is required before anger can exist."},{"t":"first movements","d":"Involuntary stirrings of mind or body that precede and may grow into passion."}]},{"i":36,"j":50,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (2 of 6)","title":"Should the wise man be angry at vice?","summary":"An adversary claims virtue should be angry at baseness; Seneca replies that the wise man would then be angry constantly and in proportion, making him irascible and miserable. He surveys the Forum, courts and circus as full of vice, quotes verses on universal treachery, and adds civil war and proscription, concluding that matching anger to crime would mean madness. Since error is universal and no one is born wise, the wise man pardons the human race, treating sinners as a physician treats patients or a sailor a leaking ship; he notes Heraclitus who wept and Democritus who laughed. Against the claim that anger usefully inspires fear, Seneca answers that the feared live in fear, citing Laberius's verse.","ideas":["Virtue does not imitate vice in repressing it","So many sinners make anger at vice both unjust and endless","To be human is a better excuse than to be a child","Whoever is feared by many must fear many"],"terms":[{"t":"Heraclitus and Democritus","d":"Philosophers who, in the anecdote, wept and laughed at human folly; Seneca prefers either to anger."},{"t":"Laberius","d":"A writer of mimes whose line on fear was applauded in the theatre during the civil war."}]},{"i":51,"j":58,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (3 of 6)","title":"Anger can be removed; objections answered","summary":"Seneca rejects the claim that anger cannot be removed while wickedness exists: as people endure cold and heat, and as training lets men walk ropes or dive deep, the mind can tame any passion, and the prize here is a calm life. The path to virtue is level and easy, while vices are laborious. Anger may be feigned to rouse dull hearers, but real anger should be suppressed, as boxers strike by opportunity rather than rage. He answers further claims: the anger of Germans and Scythians shows untrained vigour, not nobility; animal models do not suit reasoning man; irritable men seem simple only beside swindlers; orators do better by feigning anger than feeling it.","ideas":["Discipline can conquer even the fiercest passions","Virtue is easy; vice costs great effort","Feigned anger can serve where real anger harms","Bold natures incline to anger until trained"],"terms":[{"t":"feigned anger","d":"An assumed show of anger that Seneca allows for rousing others, as actors and orators do."}]},{"i":59,"j":66,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (4 of 6)","title":"Remedies begin: temperament, education, suspicion","summary":"Turning to remedies, Seneca divides them into preventing anger and avoiding wrongdoing in anger, beginning with the young. He explains proneness to anger by the mixture of hot, cold, dry and moist, and prescribes measures such as limiting wine, food and fatigue. Boys must be raised neither servile nor indulged, kept from flattery and luxury, given even-tempered tutors, and taught to win without harming. For adults, the cause of anger is the belief that one is wronged, so belief should be slow; examples include the tyrant Hippias tricked into killing his own friends, Alexander trusting his physician Philip, and Caesar burning letters to Pompeius. Luxury breeds anger over trifles, as with Mindyrides of Sybaris.","ideas":["Temperament, from the mixture of elements, inclines people to anger","Education must guide a boy's spirit between servility and arrogance","Postpone belief: suspicion and calumny feed anger","A soft life makes trifles feel like injuries"],"terms":[{"t":"paedagogus","d":"The slave-attendant who supervised a Roman boy, whose temper Seneca says the boy will copy."},{"t":"four qualities","d":"Hot, cold, dry and moist, whose mixture Seneca uses to explain differences of temper."}]},{"i":67,"j":72,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (5 of 6)","title":"Whom not to be angry with; delay","summary":"Seneca sorts the objects of anger: inanimate things and animals cannot do wrong; the gods neither wish nor are able to harm; good parents, teachers and judges hurt us for our benefit. No one is faultless, and innocence by the letter of the law is narrow, so we should judge others as we would wish to be judged, remembering that we see others' vices and not our own. The greatest remedy is delay, and reports should be tested as in a lawsuit, especially secret accusations. Anger also comes from unexpected treatment, which reflects ignorance or arrogance; expecting everything, and remembering that men are members of one larger state, should restrain the urge to harm.","ideas":["Only intentional acts are wrongs","We judge others' faults while ignoring our own","Delay is the greatest remedy for anger","Punishment should look to prevention, as limbs spare one another"],"terms":[{"t":"iniuria (wrong, injury)","d":"An intentional wrong, which Seneca says animals and things cannot commit."}]},{"i":73,"j":77,"loc":"On Anger, Book II (6 of 6)","title":"Against revenge; anger's ugliness","summary":"Seneca denies that revenge is sweet or honourable: Cato, struck in the bath, said he did not remember being hit. The wrongs of the powerful are often best borne cheerfully, as with Pastor, who dined and drank with Gaius Caesar on the day the emperor executed his son, to save his other son; Priam with Achilles is compared. One should not fight equals, superiors or inferiors, should not punish enemies' children as Sulla did, and should meet anger with kindness, since a quarrel needs two. Book II closes on anger's hideous face, a fury-like portrait, Sextius's advice to look in a mirror, and anger's power to master love, avarice and ambition.","ideas":["Retaliation differs from wrongdoing only in order","Concealing injury may be necessary under tyrants","Forgiveness turns enemies into allies, as Rome did","Anger rules over every other passion"],"terms":[{"t":"Sextius","d":"A Roman philosopher Seneca cites for the advice that the angry look at themselves in a mirror."}]},{"i":78,"j":83,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (1 of 8)","title":"Anger's violence and the plan of cure","summary":"Book III promises Novatus a way to drive out or curb anger, noting that remedies must suit each person and the strength of the fit. Anger differs from other vices: it seizes the mind suddenly, rages against every obstacle, rises from trivial causes, and alone possesses whole nations, driving mobs, senates and armies to ruin. Because Aristotle defends anger as a spur of virtue, Seneca sets out its frenzy, its instruments of torture and the angry man's face. He then states three tasks: not to become angry, to stop being angry, and to heal anger in others, and begins by comparing anger with avarice, luxury, envy and war to show it is worse.","ideas":["Anger hurls the mind headlong where other vices merely move it","Anger is the one passion that seizes whole peoples","Three aims: avoid anger, end it, cure it in others","A great mind does not feel insults; revenge admits hurt"],"terms":[{"t":"Novatus","d":"Seneca's brother, to whom On Anger is addressed."}]},{"i":84,"j":88,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (2 of 8)","title":"Avoiding anger: tasks, company, rest","summary":"The lofty mind, like the upper heavens above storms, stays calm, and Seneca cites Democritus's maxim that peace of mind requires not taking on too much. Undertakings should be measured to one's strength, since frustrated projects sour the temper. One should live among calm, straightforward people rather than the proud or quarrelsome, as Caelius, who demanded contradiction, shows; disputes should be checked early. The irascible should avoid weariness, hunger and thirst, turn to poetry, music and pleasant studies, recognize the warning signs of anger as epileptics do, and learn their own particular weak point.","ideas":["Too many or too great tasks breed vexation","Character is caught from companions","Fatigue, hunger and illness make the mind touchy","Know your own sore spot and guard it"],"terms":[{"t":"euthymia (peace of mind)","d":"The tranquillity that, in Democritus's maxim as Seneca gives it, comes from not overburdening oneself."}]},{"i":89,"j":94,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (3 of 8)","title":"Restraint, delay, concealment; tyrants' victims","summary":"Seneca advises not hearing everything and turning slights into jests, citing Socrates on a box on the ear and Pisistratus bearing a drunken guest's abuse. Much anger is self-made through suspicion, so we should consider the doer's intent, put ourselves in his place, and delay; Plato held his raised hand over a slave and later had Speusippus punish another, since he was angry. Anger should be hidden and its signs reversed, as Socrates lowered his voice. Examples show suppressed anger under tyrants: Praexaspes praising Cambyses's shot through his son's heart, and Harpagus fed his children. Seneca says death is always an open road to liberty, then urges enduring the yoke, warning kings with the cruelties of Darius and Xerxes.","ideas":["Not being inquisitive prevents much anger","Plato refused to punish while angry","Even extreme outrage can be concealed","Kings who indulge anger provoke their own fall"],"terms":[{"t":"Speusippus","d":"Plato's nephew, asked by Plato to punish a slave because Plato himself was angry."}]},{"i":95,"j":99,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (4 of 8)","title":"Examples of savage anger in rulers","summary":"Seneca turns to cruel anger even in the educated: Alexander, fresh from Aristotle, stabbed Clitus and caged Lysimachus with a lion, and Lysimachus later mutilated Telesphorus. At Rome, Sulla had Marcus Marius torn apart by Catiline, and Gaius Caesar tortured senators and knights for amusement, beheaded some by lamplight, gagged the dying, and killed their fathers. Anger also strikes nations and things: a Persian king cut off a people's noses, Cambyses marched his army into the desert against the Ethiopians until they ate one another, Cyrus split the river Gyndes into channels, and Gaius destroyed a villa at Herculaneum.","ideas":["Learning did not cure Alexander's anger","Roman cruelty rivals foreign examples","Anger lashes even rivers and buildings","Cambyses's desert march shows anger's self-destruction"],"terms":[{"t":"Gaius Caesar","d":"The emperor Caligula, Seneca's chief Roman example of cruel anger."},{"t":"Rhinocolura","d":"A Syrian place named, Seneca says, for its people's severed noses."}]},{"i":100,"j":104,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (5 of 8)","title":"Gentle rulers and the reasons to pardon","summary":"Seneca offers models of forbearance: Antigonus jokingly warned soldiers slandering him, helped men who cursed him, and laughed at insults about his looks; Philip let the rude Demochares go; Augustus merely barred Timagenes from his house and let Pollio keep him. Each provoked person should ask whether he is greater than Philip or Augustus, and should plead age, sex, friendship, or error for the offender. Remembering that the greatest also suffer harm, and our own youthful faults, makes injuries easier to bear; true magnanimity does not feel blows, like a cliff struck by waves. Since all men are bad, we must agree to forgive one another.","ideas":["Powerful men bore abuse without revenge","Every offender has some plea worth weighing","The unmoved mind stands above injury","We are bad men among bad men; mutual pardon brings peace"],"terms":[{"t":"Demochares Parrhesiastes","d":"An Athenian envoy nicknamed for his outspokenness, who told Philip to hang himself."}]},{"i":105,"j":109,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (6 of 8)","title":"Healing over revenge; envy among friends","summary":"Seneca argues that healing an injury is better than avenging it: we do not kick back at mules, and foolish men deserve the same allowance. Anger will pass on its own, so it is better to master it; meanwhile it spreads from slaves to friends to strangers and wastes life that could be spent in friendship. Many grievances are mere failures to receive, or acts done from duty to family or country, which we should respect. Slaves should be judged by whether they cannot or will not. The bitterest anger springs from disappointed expectations of friends, as when Julius Caesar was killed by friends whose hopes he could not satisfy, and from comparing our lot with others'.","ideas":["Revenge prolongs hurt; healing ends it","Distinguish refusal from injury, and inability from unwillingness","Unmet expectations turn friends into assassins","Count those below you, not those above"],"terms":[]},{"i":110,"j":116,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (7 of 8)","title":"Trifles, self-examination, and insults borne","summary":"Seneca urges a lofty view of the petty things men quarrel over: money above all, which fills the courts and divides families, then food, words, gestures and suspicions, which are children's squabbles. Masters should tolerate the talk of their household and train their ears and eyes, which bear rough sights outdoors but are fussy at home. He describes Sextius's nightly self-examination and his own practice of reviewing each day before himself. Further advice covers rude dinner jokes, insolent porters, seating at table and criticism of one's talents; the section ends with Diogenes the Stoic, spat on while lecturing on anger, and Cato's witty reply to Lentulus.","ideas":["Overvaluing trifles is the cause of anger","Desired goods are scarce, so they breed strife","Daily self-review tames anger","The mind bears what it is prepared for"],"terms":[{"t":"self-examination","d":"The nightly review of one's day, practised by Sextius and by Seneca himself."},{"t":"Diogenes","d":"Here the Stoic philosopher, who stayed calm when spat upon while lecturing."}]},{"i":117,"j":121,"loc":"On Anger, Book III (8 of 8)","title":"Soothing others' anger; remember mortality","summary":"Having dealt with our own minds, Seneca turns to calming others: wait out the first burst, then use delay, pretended sympathy, shame, fear, or distracting talk, as a physician hid a lancet in a sponge. Reproving an angry man only adds anger, unless one has Augustus's authority, who ordered Vedius Pollio's crystal broken and his lamprey tank filled to save a slave. Peace of mind comes from wholesome maxims, good deeds and a quiet conscience. The work ends by urging that anger be uprooted, not moderated, because life is short and death will soon make all equal, like a bull and bear chained together in the arena.","ideas":["Anger must be allowed to slacken before it can be soothed","Augustus and Vedius Pollio: power used to check cruelty","Anger should be eradicated, not moderated","Mortality makes quarrels a waste of brief life"],"terms":[{"t":"Vedius Pollio","d":"A wealthy friend of Augustus who tried to feed a slave to his lampreys for breaking a cup."}]},{"i":122,"j":126,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (opening)","title":"Life is not short, we waste it","summary":"After an editorial note on the essay's date and addressee, Seneca tells Paulinus that most people, including Hippocrates and Aristotle, wrongly blame Nature for the shortness of life. Life is long enough if it is well invested; it is squandered on avarice, drink, sloth, ambition, trade, war and servile attendance on the great. Men who guard their land and money give their time away freely. Seneca imagines making an old man reckon up how little of his hundred years was really his own, and attacks those who postpone living until retirement at fifty or sixty.","ideas":["Life is long if used well; we make it short by waste","The vices keep people from ever returning to themselves","People guard property but give away their time","Postponing wisdom to old age is foolish forgetfulness of mortality"],"terms":[{"t":"tempus (time)","d":"For Seneca the one possession in which it is right to be miserly."},{"t":"occupati (the engrossed)","d":"Busy people absorbed in affairs that leave them no time for themselves."}]},{"i":127,"j":135,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (1 of 4)","title":"Great men who longed for leisure","summary":"Seneca cites the powerful who longed for leisure: Augustus, who prayed for rest amid civil wars and plots, Cicero, who called himself half a prisoner at Tusculum, and Livius Drusus, who complained he had never had a holiday. Such complaints change nothing; the complainers fall back into their round. The worst wasters are those given to wine and lust. Since the divided mind learns nothing deeply, and it takes a whole life to learn to live and to die, only the man who lets none of his time be filched has a long life. Grey hairs show long existence, not long living, like a ship tossed in circles.","ideas":["Even Augustus treated future leisure as his consolation","The wise man is never 'half a prisoner'; he is above Fortune","Learning to live, and to die, takes a whole life","A long existence is not a long life"],"terms":[{"t":"otium (leisure)","d":"Time free from public and social business, which the great desire but rarely gain."}]},{"i":136,"j":143,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (2 of 4)","title":"Time's value and the three tenses","summary":"Men trifle with time because it is incorporeal and unseen, though they would spend everything to escape death. Life runs on silently without turning back, and postponement, which trades today for a promised tomorrow, is the greatest waste of it; Seneca reads Virgil's line on the fairest day fleeing first as a call to live at once. Following Fabianus, he prefers direct attack on the passions to subtle argument. He divides time into past, present and future: the past is certain and beyond Fortune, but the engrossed cannot bear to look back on it, and the present slips from them. Old men beg for more years and die in terror, while the man remote from business finds his life sufficient.","ideas":["Time is undervalued because it cannot be seen","Expectancy of tomorrow wastes today","The past is the sacred, secure part of life, closed to the engrossed","The wise man meets death with a steady step"],"terms":[{"t":"Fabianus","d":"Papirius Fabianus, an older philosopher Seneca praises for attacking the passions head-on."}]},{"i":144,"j":146,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (3 of 4)","title":"Busy idleness and useless learning","summary":"Seneca defines the engrossed broadly: even men at leisure in their villas are busy with trifles, such as collectors of Corinthian bronzes, men debating each hair at the barber's, singers, and hosts fussing over banquets. The pampered man who asked whether he was now seated is not at leisure but half dead. Also wasted are those who study useless literary and antiquarian questions, now spreading from Greeks to Romans: the number of Ulysses' rowers, the first Roman naval victory, the origin of names. Pompey's elephant slaughter shows such firsts can be shameful; Seneca asks whom these facts make braver or more just.","ideas":["Leisure filled with trifles is 'idle occupation', not leisure","Luxury can make a man unaware even of his own posture","Antiquarian curiosity adds nothing to virtue","Pompey's spectacle shows the blindness great prosperity brings"],"terms":[{"t":"desidiosa occupatio (busy idleness)","d":"Seneca's name for leisure spent on fussy, pointless pursuits."},{"t":"pomerium","d":"Rome's sacred boundary, one of the antiquarian topics Seneca mocks."}]},{"i":147,"j":153,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (4 of 4)","title":"Philosophy joins all ages to one life","summary":"Only those who take time for philosophy are truly at leisure and truly live, because they annex every past age: they may argue with Socrates, doubt with Carneades, or find peace with Epicurus. Unlike great patrons whose doors are shut to clients, Zeno, Pythagoras, Aristotle and the rest are always at home and send visitors away richer. One may choose to be adopted into these households of great minds, whose legacy grows when shared and leads to immortality. By contrast, those who forget the past, neglect the present and fear the future have brief, troubled lives; their days drag while their pleasures are anxious, as Xerxes wept over his army's mortality.","ideas":["The philosopher lengthens life by combining all times into one","The great thinkers are friends who never turn a visitor away","We cannot choose our parents but can choose our intellectual family","Pleasures built on chance are uneasy and always fear their end"],"terms":[{"t":"philosophia","d":"Presented here as the only real use of leisure and the way to share in all ages."}]},{"i":154,"j":157,"loc":"On the Shortness of Life (conclusion)","title":"Paulinus urged to retire to study","summary":"Seneca urges Paulinus, overseer of Rome's grain supply, to leave public office for a peaceful harbour. His work is honourable but suited to plodding oxen rather than thoroughbreds, and it deals with a hungry people, as the near-famine after Gaius Caesar's reign showed. Retirement should mean higher studies: the nature of God, the fate of the soul, and the order of the cosmos, along with virtue and knowledge of living and dying. The essay closes by pitying those who labour at others' business and die in harness, like Sextus Turannius, who had himself mourned when retired, and those who plan tombs while forgetting death.","ideas":["Better to know the ledger of one's own life than of the corn-market","True retirement is a turn to natural and moral philosophy, not sloth","High office is bought at the price of life","Men find it harder to get leisure from themselves than from the law"],"terms":[{"t":"praefectus annonae","d":"The official in charge of Rome's grain supply, the post Paulinus held."}]},{"i":158,"j":184,"loc":"The Pumpkinification of the Divine Claudius 1–5","title":"Claudius dies and arrives in heaven","summary":"After notes on the satire's authorship and title, the narrator claims free speech now that Claudius is dead, citing as his source the official who swore he saw Drusilla ascend. Mock-epic verses set the date, 13 October. Mercury persuades Clotho to end Claudius' life; she cuts his thread while Lachesis and Apollo spin a long golden life for Nero. Claudius dies listening to comic actors, his last words about having fouled himself. In heaven Jupiter sends Hercules to identify a limping, mumbling stranger, who answers in Homeric verse, claiming to come from Troy.","ideas":["Mock-historical claim to truth and a burlesque source","The Fates end Claudius' life and Apollo praises Nero","Claudius' undignified death","Hercules mistakes Claudius for a monster, a thirteenth labour"],"terms":[{"t":"apocolocyntosis (pumpkinification)","d":"A parody of apotheosis, the formal deification of a dead emperor."},{"t":"Menippean satire","d":"A satiric form mixing prose and verse, of which this is the sole surviving classical example."}]},{"i":185,"j":205,"loc":"The Pumpkinification of the Divine Claudius 6–11","title":"The gods debate Claudius' deification","summary":"Fever, who came with Claudius, reveals that he is a Gaul born at Lugudunum; he orders her beheaded but no one heeds him. Hercules, playing a tragedian, threatens him, and Claudius appeals to him as his old patron. After a gap in the text, a speaker mocks the idea of making him a god of any school. In the heavenly senate Janus moves that no more mortals be deified, while Diespiter moves for Claudius' godhead, and Hercules lobbies for votes. Then Augustus speaks, listing Claudius' murders of his own kin and others, and his resolution that Claudius be expelled from heaven within thirty days carries; Mercury drags him down.","ideas":["Fever exposes Claudius' Gallic birth","The heavenly senate parodies Roman procedure","Augustus' indictment lists Claudius' killings","Such gods would destroy belief in the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"Conscript Fathers","d":"The formal address to the Roman senate, here used for the assembly of gods."}]},{"i":206,"j":215,"loc":"The Pumpkinification of the Divine Claudius 12–15","title":"Descent to Hades and Claudius' trial","summary":"On the Via Sacra, Claudius sees his own funeral, where the people rejoice like free men and only pettifoggers mourn; a mock dirge praises his conquests and his habit of judging after hearing one side. Mercury takes him down to the underworld, where Narcissus announces him and his many victims, led by Messalina's circle and his own relatives, greet him. Before Aeacus he is charged with killing thirty-five senators and 221 knights, and is condemned without a defence hearing. His punishment is to gamble forever with a bottomless dice-box, until Gaius Caesar claims him as a slave and he is handed over as a freedman's law-clerk.","ideas":["Rome celebrates Claudius' funeral","His victims welcome him in Hades","Aeacus judges him as he judged others, hearing one side","An endless, futile punishment with a bottomless dice-box"],"terms":[{"t":"Aeacus","d":"Judge of the dead, who tries Claudius under the Cornelian law on murder."}]},{"i":216,"j":222,"loc":"On the Firmness of the Wise Person (1 of 3)","title":"The wise man cannot be injured","summary":"Seneca tells Serenus that the Stoics take the steep but direct road. Serenus had been indignant at the mob's treatment of Cato; Seneca replies that Cato, a truer model of wisdom than Ulysses or Hercules, could suffer neither injury nor insult. Serenus objects that Stoic paradoxes merely rename ordinary hardships. Seneca answers that the invulnerable is not what is never struck but what is never wounded: the wise man is like adamant or a cliff against waves. Attacks fall short of him as arrows shot at the sun.","ideas":["Cato as the pattern of the Stoic wise man","Objection: the Stoic claims only change the names of things","The invulnerable is never wounded, though struck","Wisdom shows best in calm amid attacks"],"terms":[{"t":"iniuria (injury)","d":"A wrong that does real harm, which Seneca says cannot reach the wise man."},{"t":"sapiens (the wise man)","d":"The Stoic ideal of the perfected person, secure against Fortune."}]},{"i":223,"j":228,"loc":"On the Firmness of the Wise Person (2 of 3)","title":"Why injury cannot reach virtue","summary":"Seneca distinguishes injury from insult and argues the first part: since the only evil is baseness, and virtue leaves no room for it, no injury can reach the wise man, who has entrusted nothing to fortune. The philosopher Stilbo, having lost home and daughters when Demetrius took Megara, said he had lost nothing. Seneca adds that the wise man exists though rarely, that the weaker cannot hurt the stronger, and that an injury may be done without being received, as with Socrates' condemnation. The wise man treats men's wrongs like weather, lives without hope or fear, and uses attacks to test his virtue.","ideas":["No evil without baseness, so no injury to the virtuous","Stilbo: all my property is with me","An injury can be done yet not received","Death is not an evil, so lesser losses are easily borne"],"terms":[{"t":"contumelia (insult)","d":"The lesser form of injury, which angers without wounding."},{"t":"Stilbo (Stilpo)","d":"Megarian philosopher whose reply to Demetrius Poliorcetes illustrates inner security."}]},{"i":229,"j":233,"loc":"On the Firmness of the Wise Person (3 of 3)","title":"The wise man and insult","summary":"Turning to the second part, Seneca argues that the wise man cannot be insulted. Insult troubles only weak and pampered spirits, though the wise man does feel real blows such as pain and bereavement, which he overcomes. Insult implies contempt, which inferiors cannot truly show; adults with childish minds are treated like children, and the wise man punishes them only to correct them. Like a physician with raving patients, he cares neither for the respect nor the scorn of rich men or kings. He pays a surly porter's fee like a toll and, like Cato when struck, ignores rather than forgives the blow.","ideas":["The wise man feels real pain but not petty slights","Contumelia is derived from contempt","Correction, not revenge, is why the wise man punishes","Cato ignored a blow rather than pardoning it"],"terms":[{"t":"magnanimitas (greatness of mind)","d":"The virtue that passes over insults as unreal apparitions."}]},{"i":234,"j":238,"loc":"On the Firmness of the Wise Person (conclusion)","title":"Stoics, Epicurus and enduring mockery","summary":"Seneca insists that if small affronts do not affect the wise man, neither will great ones. Even Epicurus said Fortune seldom hinders the wise man; the Stoics go further and deny that injuries exist for him, while not denying pain. Both schools urge contempt for insults, and Seneca gives the sensible man's reasoning: a deserved reproach is a sentence, an undeserved one shames its author. Examples follow: men undone by silly jibes, Vatinius disarming mockery by mocking himself, Caligula's insults to Valerius Asiaticus and Chaerea that led to his murder, and Socrates and Antisthenes bearing jibes calmly. True freedom is to raise the mind above injuries and hold one's post as a man.","ideas":["Stoics and Epicureans differ little in practice on injury","Laughing at oneself disarms the insulter","Caligula's insults provoked Chaerea's fatal blow","Freedom is inner superiority to injury, not immunity from it"],"terms":[{"t":"Epicurus","d":"Cited as conceding that Fortune seldom troubles the wise man."}]},{"i":239,"j":246,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (opening)","title":"A mirror for Nero's clemency","summary":"Editorial notes describe the work's date, address to Nero, and reception. Seneca offers the book as a mirror in which Nero may see himself, imagining Nero's soliloquy as arbiter of life and death who keeps his sword sheathed and spares even the lowest. He praises Nero's innocence of bloodshed, the peace of his reign, and the hope his clemency gives to all. Answering those who think clemency only shelters villains, he argues it also helps the unfortunate and reclaims the reformable, but must follow a middle course. He divides the subject into gentleness, the nature of clemency, and how the mind may acquire it.","ideas":["The ruler as a god on earth who restrains his power","Nero's reign praised as a pattern needing no model","Pardoning all is as cruel as pardoning none","Three-part plan of the treatise"],"terms":[{"t":"clementia (clemency)","d":"Mildness in the use of power to punish, the virtue Seneca commends to Nero."}]},{"i":247,"j":255,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (1 of 4)","title":"Why clemency best befits a ruler","summary":"Clemency, the most humane virtue, suits all men but especially princes, since power is glorious only when beneficent. The people defend the ruler as the body serves the mind, and Rome would fall apart without its head; so the prince who spares citizens spares his own body. To save life is a godlike privilege, and since all have sinned, a strict judge would empty the city. The prince should treat his subjects as he would wish the gods to treat him. Because he lives in full view like the sun, and his anger shakes everything, he lacks the private man's freedom, and cruelty multiplies his enemies.","ideas":["The ruler is the soul of the state, the state his body","Anyone may kill unlawfully; only the ruler can save","All of us have sinned and will sin","A prince's high station is a kind of slavery shared with the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"animal sociale (social animal)","d":"The Stoic view of man, born for the good of the whole community."}]},{"i":256,"j":259,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (2 of 4)","title":"Augustus pardons Cinna","summary":"Seneca tells how the aged Augustus, learning of Lucius Cinna's plot, spent a troubled night until Livia advised trying clemency after so many failed executions. Augustus summoned Cinna, recounted his past kindnesses and the details of the plot in a speech of two hours, then granted him his life a second time and later the consulship; Cinna became loyal and no one plotted again. Augustus' clemency toward former enemies won him safety and a reputation as a god. But Seneca notes that this came after Actium, Perusia and the proscriptions, and calls it weariness of cruelty, praising Nero's clemency as unstained from the start.","ideas":["Livia: when harsh remedies fail, try their opposite","Pardon turned an enemy into a loyal friend","Augustus' mercy came late, after bloodshed","Nero's unstained clemency is ranked above it"],"terms":[]},{"i":260,"j":268,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (3 of 4)","title":"King versus tyrant; the father's model","summary":"Clemency distinguishes king from tyrant: both may kill, but the king does so only for the public good, while the tyrant, like Sulla, delights in cruelty and lives by the motto 'let them hate, if they fear.' Excessive terror drives men to desperate courage, and the tyrant fears his own guards and must keep committing crimes. The good prince, by contrast, is guarded by love. His model is the father, who punishes reluctantly; the story of Tarius, who exiled his would-be parricide son with Augustus as an impartial assessor, shows this. Teachers, officers, horse-breakers and physicians all succeed by gentleness, and even slaves should be treated with justice, unlike Vedius Pollio.","ideas":["The tyrant differs from the king in deeds, not title","Fear in excess breeds desperate resistance","'Father of his Country' implies a father's mildness","Vice is a disease needing a patient physician"],"terms":[{"t":"pater patriae (Father of his Country)","d":"A title Seneca reads as entrusting the ruler with a father's gentle authority."}]},{"i":269,"j":273,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (4 of 4)","title":"The stingless king bee and punishment","summary":"Nature models kingship in the bees, whose king alone has no sting, a lesson in restraint for rulers. The king is safe only when his subjects are safe, and the love of citizens is a fortress that cannot be stormed. Seneca then treats the reasons for punishment. For wrongs to himself, a prince should forgo or moderate revenge, since he needs no compensation and sparing a fallen enemy adds to his glory. For wrongs to others, law aims to correct, deter, or remove the wicked; mild and rare punishment serves these best, as frequent penalties make crime familiar.","ideas":["The king bee is stingless by nature's design","Clemency makes a king safe even on open ground","Sparing a defeated enemy is a triumph over one's own victory","Frequent punishment weakens its effect"],"terms":[{"t":"rex apium (king of the bees)","d":"Seneca's natural example of a ruler without a weapon of revenge."}]},{"i":274,"j":276,"loc":"On Clemency, Book I (conclusion)","title":"The madness and danger of cruelty","summary":"Laws against parricide, Seneca says, seemed to teach the crime, and a proposal to give slaves distinctive dress was dropped lest they count their numbers; likewise showing how many are guilty is dangerous, and many executions shame a ruler as many funerals shame a physician. Cruelty is bestial, as in Alexander's casting of Lysimachus to a lion, and is the worst vice because it turns killing into pleasure. A cruel ruler, like a monstrous serpent or a spreading fire, provokes a general rising, and his kingdom resembles a captured city. The truest glory is the civic crown for saving citizens' lives.","ideas":["Punishment can advertise the crimes it punishes","Cruelty goes beyond human limits into bestial pleasure","Widespread cruelty unites all against the tyrant","Saving lives is godlike; mass killing is ruin"],"terms":[{"t":"corona civica (civic crown)","d":"The honour for saving citizens' lives, which Seneca calls the fittest ornament for a prince."}]},{"i":277,"j":285,"loc":"On Clemency, Book II","title":"Mercy defined against cruelty and pity","summary":"Seneca recalls Nero's reluctant exclamation, when asked to sign two death warrants, that he wished he had never learned his letters, and hopes his goodness will spread through the empire and bring back a golden age. He then defines mercy as the mind's restraint from vengeance when it has power to take it, or as leniency in punishing. Its opposite is not strictness but cruelty, excess in exacting penalties; killing for pleasure is rather ferocity or madness. Pity, a disorder of weak minds, is to be avoided: the wise man helps the afflicted with a calm mind. Nor does he pardon, since pardon remits a due penalty, but mercy freely judges by equity; Seneca calls the difference a dispute about words.","ideas":["Nero's saying as a sign of natural gentleness","No virtue is opposed to another; cruelty opposes mercy","Pity is a sickness; the wise man helps without grief","Mercy judges by equity, pardon omits a due penalty"],"terms":[{"t":"misericordia (pity)","d":"Sorrow at others' misfortunes, which the Stoics count a vice of weak minds."},{"t":"venia (pardon)","d":"Remission of a deserved punishment, which the Stoic wise man does not grant."}]},{"i":286,"j":304,"loc":"On the Happy Life (opening)","title":"The crowd misleads; defining the highest good","summary":"After an editorial introduction and the translator's preface, Seneca tells his brother Gallio that all men want to live happily but take the wrong road by following the crowd and common rumour instead of reason. He sets aside other schools and, while claiming the right to his own judgment within Stoicism, defines the happy life as one in accordance with nature, resting on a sound, steadfast and courageous mind. He offers several equivalent definitions of the highest good: a mind that despises fortune and delights in virtue, free of fear and desire, counting only honour good and blame bad. He ends by arguing that animals and dull men lack happiness because they lack reason, and that a mind feasting on remembered and hoped-for pleasures is insane, not happy.","ideas":["The majority is the worst guide to the good life","Happiness is living according to nature under reason","The highest good can be stated in many forms with one meaning","Freedom from pleasure and pain comes through contempt of Fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"summum bonum (highest good)","d":"The final end of life, which Seneca locates in a virtuous mind rather than in pleasure."},{"t":"secundum naturam (according to nature)","d":"The Stoic standard on which Seneca says all the school agrees: conduct moulded on nature's law."}]},{"i":305,"j":314,"loc":"On the Happy Life (middle)","title":"Virtue must lead and pleasure follow","summary":"Seneca attacks those who say pleasure and virtue are inseparable, contrasting virtue, found in the temple and on the walls, with pleasure, found in baths and taverns. Virtue is sought for herself and is her own reward; pleasure comes as a by-product, like flowers in a field ploughed for corn. Against an Epicurean adversary he argues that a good needing virtue as its guardian is a poor highest good, and that luxurious men such as Nomentanus and Apicius have pleasure without goodness. He grants that Epicurus's own teaching was sober and holy, but says its name gives cover to vice. He concludes that virtue must carry the standard, that a man must follow God willingly, and that perfect virtue suffices for happiness while those still progressing drag a looser chain.","ideas":["Pleasure is a by-product of virtue, not its cause or reward","Epicurus's doctrine is upright but its label invites abuse","Liberty lies in obeying God and accepting what nature brings","The sage needs nothing from fortune; the learner still does"],"terms":[{"t":"voluptas (pleasure)","d":"Treated as low, perishable and a poor master, at best a companion following virtue."},{"t":"deum sequi (follow God)","d":"The old maxim Seneca invokes for willing acceptance of the universe's order."}]},{"i":315,"j":324,"loc":"On the Happy Life (later chapters)","title":"Answering the charge of hypocrisy","summary":"Seneca imagines critics who ask why philosophers who praise poverty live in comfort, own estates and grieve at deaths. He answers that he is not a wise man but one trying to lessen his vices daily, and that the same reproach was cast at Plato, Epicurus, Zeno, Rutilius, Cato and even Diogenes. He praises the Epicurean Diodorus, who recently took his own life, and sets out the noble rules a philosopher proposes for himself, such as viewing death and comedy alike and living for others. Those who attempt great things deserve respect even if they fall short. He then argues that the wise man may keep wealth without fearing to lose it, since riches are among the preferred indifferents that give virtue wider scope.","ideas":["A student of wisdom is not yet a sage and need not match his words","Lofty aims deserve honour even when unfulfilled","The wise man owns riches without being owned by them","Indifferent things can still be preferable"],"terms":[{"t":"proficiens (one making progress)","d":"The person on the road to virtue whom Seneca speaks for, as opposed to the perfect sage."},{"t":"preferred indifferents","d":"Things neither good nor bad, like wealth and health, which nonetheless have value and may be chosen."}]},{"i":325,"j":330,"loc":"On the Happy Life (closing chapters)","title":"Honest wealth, wise giving, and Socrates' rebuke","summary":"Seneca says no one has condemned wisdom to poverty: the philosopher may hold great wealth if it is honestly won, openly kept and wisely given. Giving is difficult, requires choosing worthy recipients, and can be practised toward slaves as well as free men. Distinguishing the learner from the perfected sage, he has the sage explain that riches are not good, since bad men have them, but are desirable, and that he would rather regulate joys than stifle sorrows. Some virtues need the spur and others the curb. The work ends with Socrates speaking from his prison, scorning the critics who probe philosophers' houses and dinners while they themselves are covered in ulcers and threatened by storms they cannot see.","ideas":["Wealth must be gained and spent without wronging anyone","Riches are slaves in the wise man's house, masters in the fool's","Uphill virtues (patience, courage) versus downhill ones (generosity, moderation)","Attacks only show virtue's strength, like waves on a rock"],"terms":[{"t":"liberalitas (freehandedness)","d":"Generosity, which Seneca says takes its name from a free-born mind, not from being owed to free men."}]},{"i":331,"j":341,"loc":"On Leisure","title":"Why a Stoic may choose retirement","summary":"The fragmentary dialogue opens with Seneca praising retirement as a refuge from the mob's changing opinions, then answering an objector who accuses him of preaching Epicurus inside Zeno's camp. He argues that a man may devote himself to contemplation either from the start of life or after his service is done, and that Stoics as well as Epicureans send the wise man to leisure when the state is past helping or he lacks strength. There are two commonwealths, the universal one of gods and men and the one of birth, and the greater can be served in leisure by inquiring into nature and God. Nature made man both to contemplate and to act; Zeno, Cleanthes and Chrysippus served mankind more by their writings than by office. Since no actual state can bear the wise man, leisure becomes necessary for all.","ideas":["Two republics: the world-city and the city of birth","Contemplation is itself a kind of action and service","Nature placed man upright to be a spectator of her works","No existing state is fit for the wise man"],"terms":[{"t":"otium (leisure)","d":"Retirement from public affairs devoted to study and the pursuit of virtue."},{"t":"the three lives","d":"The lives of pleasure, contemplation and action, which Seneca argues overlap rather than exclude one another."}]},{"i":342,"j":371,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book I, Preface","title":"Why study the heavens: the soul's ascent","summary":"An editorial introduction describes the Natural Questions as a seven-book survey of nature addressed to Lucilius, followed by a list of the books' contents. In the preface proper, Seneca distinguishes the part of philosophy concerned with human conduct from the higher part that studies heaven and God. Escaping vice is not yet achievement; the soul's true felicity is to rise above the earth and survey the universe. From that height kingdoms and frontiers look like the plots of ants on a threshing-floor. There the soul, akin to the divine, learns that God is universal reason, wholly mind, and asks whether God creates matter or works on what is given.","ideas":["Natural philosophy rises above ethics as heaven above earth","Seen from the stars, human empires are tiny","The soul's delight in the divine proves its divine origin","God is reason, within and without his works"],"terms":[{"t":"ratio (reason)","d":"What God wholly is, according to Seneca, whereas in humans it is only the better part."}]},{"i":372,"j":385,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book I (meteors, halos)","title":"Meteors, shooting stars and halos","summary":"Seneca turns to the fires seen crossing the sky, including Aristotle's 'She-Goat' and the fireballs recorded before the deaths of Augustus, Sejanus and Germanicus, postponing the question of whether they are omens. He explains them as produced by violent friction of the atmosphere or by fiery exhalations from the earth kindled in the clouds, and denies that real stars fall. Lights settling on sails, taken by sailors as Castor and Pollux, signal a storm breaking. He then explains the halo or crown: like ripples from a stone in a pond, it forms when light strikes still, dense air, close to the earth, not near the heavenly bodies. How a halo breaks up foretells calm, wind from one quarter, or a storm.","ideas":["Meteors arise from atmospheric friction or kindled earthly exhalations","Day-time meteors exist but are hidden by sunlight","Halos form in still air like ripples on a pond","Halos are weather signs, more often seen at night"],"terms":[{"t":"halo (corona)","d":"A coloured ring round sun or moon, which the Greeks also called a threshing-floor."}]},{"i":386,"j":403,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book I (rainbow)","title":"The rainbow: mirror or coloured cloud?","summary":"Seneca reviews theories of the rainbow, which needs both sun and cloud and always appears opposite the sun. Some explain it by light and shade in drops; others, with Aristotle, by countless raindrops each mirroring the sun and blending into one image, which Seneca defends against the objection that the eye sees no images, since sight is easily deceived. Opponents reply that clouds are not mirrors, that combined mirrors give many images not one, and that colours like a dove's neck change without any mirror, so the bow is a cloud dyed by the sun. Posidonius answers that real colour would grow clearer nearer at hand. Seneca rejects that argument but sides with Posidonius: the bow is a distorted image of the sun in a hollow cloud.","ideas":["The bow requires sun and cloud and lies opposite the sun","Raindrops as countless small mirrors","The coloured-cloud theory and its objections","Distorting mirrors explain an image that does not resemble the sun"],"terms":[{"t":"speculum (mirror)","d":"The model for the rainbow theory Seneca favours, in which the cloud reflects an image of the sun."}]},{"i":404,"j":421,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book I (rainbow, streaks, mock suns)","title":"Rainbow details, streaks and mock suns","summary":"Seneca notes that the rainbow is never more than a semicircle and signals weather by its quarter, and argues that its sudden appearance and vanishing, its dependence on the sun, and its enlarged size all suit a mirror image. He meets the objection from fluted glass rods, which throw rainbow colours, by saying they are imperfect mirrors. He rejects explanations of the semicircular shape that rely on the sun striking only the tops of clouds, and reports Aristotle on the seasons and hours of rainbows. Streaks are straight, incomplete rainbows near the sun; mock suns, seen in twos and threes, are images of the sun in dense, smooth clouds, as the sun is seen in basins of oil during eclipses. Both portend rain.","ideas":["Sudden emergence and extinction point to reflection","Halo, rainbow and streak as circle, half-circle and line","Mock suns are mirrored suns, sometimes images of images","Viewing eclipses in basins of oil or pitch"],"terms":[{"t":"parhelion (mock sun)","d":"A bright image of the sun formed beside it in a thick cloud, by reflection."}]},{"i":422,"j":436,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book I (fires, mirrors)","title":"Celestial fires, Hostius Quadra, and mirrors","summary":"Seneca lists other celestial fires: pit-shaped and barrel-shaped meteors, chasms, shooting stars whose speed makes a trail, gleams that can strike houses, and the glow under Tiberius that sent firemen to Ostia. Unlike these, rainbows and halos are mere reflections without substance. He then tells of Hostius Quadra, a rich debauchee murdered by his slaves, who used magnifying mirrors to watch his own vice, as an example of lust abusing nature's gifts. Nature made mirrors so that we could view the sun safely, study eclipses, and know ourselves. Early people saw themselves in streams and plain metal; luxury later produced jewelled full-length mirrors costing more than the dowries the Senate gave Scipio's daughters.","ideas":["Meteoric fires have substance; rainbows and halos do not","Hostius Quadra's mirrored chamber as lust perverting nature","The mirror's purposes: observing the sun and knowing oneself","The decline from simple reflection to costly luxury"],"terms":[{"t":"sela (luminosities)","d":"Greek name for brief bright gleams in the sky, sometimes as damaging as lightning."}]},{"i":437,"j":452,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (opening)","title":"Divisions of physics and the nature of air","summary":"Book II opens by dividing natural inquiry into the study of heavenly bodies, of the atmosphere between heaven and earth, and of the earth; earthquakes belong with the atmosphere because air causes them. Seneca argues that the air is a single continuous body possessing unity, not a heap of atoms with void between, and that it is both a part of the world and the medium carrying earth's nourishment to the stars. Its tension is shown by inflated objects, the voice, the force of growing roots, and water jets, and birds pass through it by displacement as through water. The air has three layers: a hot dry upper layer, a cold middle layer, and a changeable lower layer warmed by the earth. These remarks prepare for thunder and lightning.","ideas":["Three divisions: celestial, meteorological, terrestrial","Air is a unified body held by tension","Air joins heaven and earth while separating them","The three temperature layers of the atmosphere"],"terms":[{"t":"intentio (tension)","d":"The Stoic tensile force in air that gives bodies cohesion, strength and motion."},{"t":"peristasis (displacement)","d":"Air or water closing round a moving body, explaining motion without void."}]},{"i":453,"j":465,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (theories of lightning)","title":"Theories of thunder and lightning","summary":"Seneca separates sheet lightning, which shows fire, from the thunderbolt, which hurls it, and surveys disputed theories: fire stored in clouds or produced on the spot, Anaxagoras's sparks from the ether, and Aristotle's dry earthly exhalation forced out between clouds with a noise. He rejects the idea that fire falls from the ether, whose ordered fire cannot be dislodged, though the upper air may be kindled by its heat. He reports the views of Stoics, Anaximenes, Anaximander, Anaxagoras and Diogenes of Apollonia. Then, walking alone, he holds it agreed that flash and bolt are the same fire differing only in force, and that clouds, like flint or rubbed wood, make fire by blow or friction.","ideas":["Flash and bolt differ in force, not in kind","Ether's fire does not fall but may kindle the air beneath","Survey of Presocratic accounts of thunder","Fire produced by striking or by friction"],"terms":[{"t":"fulmen / fulguratio","d":"The thunderbolt that strikes and the lightning flash that only shines."}]},{"i":466,"j":476,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (thunder)","title":"Objections answered; kinds of thunder","summary":"Seneca answers objections: fire naturally rises but can be forced downward like a bent branch, and moist clouds can still yield fire, since cloud is thick air not yet water. As proof he cites Posidonius's account of a volcanic island rising from the Aegean, where fire broke through the sea itself. Lightning comes only when rain threatens; flashes on a starry night come from clouds out of view. Some distinguish a deep rumbling thunder from a sharp peal like a bursting bladder, which can kill or derange. Clouds striking mountains make no sound because they are dispersed, not burst, and Asclepiodotus holds that dry bodies like Etna's ash or desert sand may also produce thunder.","ideas":["Forced motion is no sign of an element's nature","Fire can overcome water, as in the Aegean eruption","Rumbling thunder versus sharp peals","Sound needs a sudden burst of enclosed air"],"terms":[]},{"i":477,"j":487,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (omens, fate)","title":"Lightning's wonders, omens, and fate","summary":"Seneca lists strange effects showing divine power in lightning: coins melted in an unhurt purse, wine frozen in a shattered jar, venom removed from serpents. Lightning also foretells the future, and he contrasts the Romans, who think lightning signifies because clouds collide, with the Etruscans, who think clouds collide in order to signify. Everything in the chain of fate is a sign of what follows, though only some signs are observed. He denies that lightning can cancel other true omens, since truth is one, and denies that it changes fate, which is unbreakable necessity. Yet vows and expiations work because they too are included in fate, as the doctor is its instrument in a cure.","ideas":["Etruscan versus Roman view of lightning's purpose","Any event in a series can be a sign","One true omen cannot overrule another","Prayers and expiation are themselves part of fate"],"terms":[{"t":"fatum (fate)","d":"The binding necessity of all events, which no sacrifice can alter."},{"t":"procuratio (expiation)","d":"Rites to avert portended evils, which Seneca says work because they are fated."}]},{"i":488,"j":503,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (kinds of lightning)","title":"Classifying lightning: Caecina, Etruscans, Attalus","summary":"Seneca reports Caecina's three kinds of lightning, counselling, authoritative and ordinary, and the physical kinds that bore, split or scorch. The Etruscans assign Jupiter three bolts, one sent by himself, one on advice of twelve gods, one with the veiled supreme gods. Seneca argues the ancients were not foolish: they used fear of an armed avenger to restrain the ignorant, and taught rulers to take counsel before harming and to vary punishments. Their Jupiter is the ruler and soul of the universe, rightly called fate, providence, nature or the world, who gives every bolt its cause without directing each. He rejects a division into constant, limited and deferred bolts, lists Caecina's many names, and prefers the simpler scheme of the Stoic Attalus.","ideas":["Etruscan doctrine of Jupiter's three bolts","Ancient myths as tools to teach fear and counsel","Jupiter as fate, providence, nature and world","Attalus's division by meaning for us"],"terms":[{"t":"Etruscan lightning lore","d":"The Tuscan science of observing, interpreting and expiating lightning."},{"t":"providentia (providence)","d":"One of the names Seneca gives Jupiter as the plan by which the world runs its course."}]},{"i":504,"j":515,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book II (close)","title":"Seneca's own account and the fear of death","summary":"Seneca describes how lightning's force varies with material, shattering hard things, sparing soft ones, and leaving poison in frozen wine and a sulphur smell. He returns to Posidonius's view that thunder is the explosion of dry exhalations bursting from clouds, rejects Clidemus's claim that lightning is a mere reflection, and notes Heraclitus and Caecina's remarks on old Latin usage. His own view: a flash is air turned to fire by rarefaction, and a bolt is the same fire with more fuel, zigzag because nature draws it up and violence drives it down. Granting Lucilius's wish for a moral, he urges contempt of death: all are sentenced to die, lightning is only the most conspicuous danger, and no one struck by it ever feared it.","ideas":["Lightning's effects depend on the resistance of the material","A bolt is lightning in its most intense form","Study of nature should free the mind from fear","Death is the common sentence; lightning is no special threat"],"terms":[]},{"i":516,"j":528,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, Preface","title":"Late start on a vast inquiry into nature","summary":"Seneca tells Lucilius that in old age he has begun the huge task of surveying the universe, and must make up for wasted years by hard work. He contrasts this with historians who record the crimes of kings such as Philip, Alexander and Hannibal, and argues it is better to cure one's own ills and to teach that fortune's gifts are unstable. Repeatedly asking what is the principal thing in life, he answers: to grasp the universe in thought, to master one's vices, to rise above fortune, to bear adversity gladly as God's decree, and to hold life ready to give up. The study of nature, he concludes, lifts the mind above what is base and frees the spirit from the body.","ideas":["Toil in old age must repair a lifetime spent on vain pursuits","Studying the works of the gods is better than chronicling conquerors","The principal thing is mastery of self and indifference to fortune","True freedom is escape from slavery to oneself"],"terms":[{"t":"fortuna (fortune)","d":"The changeable power whose gifts Seneca says are more fickle than the air and must not be trusted."}]},{"i":529,"j":550,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, chs. 1–14","title":"Kinds of water and where rivers come from","summary":"Seneca sets out his plan to study terrestrial waters, setting the Nile aside for later, and classifies waters by motion, taste, temperature, weight, colour and effect. He reviews theories of why rivers never fail: sea water filtered back through the earth, rainfall (which he rejects from his own digging in his vineyards and from springs on mountain tops), and vast underground reservoirs. He favours the Stoic view that underground air condenses into water and that the elements change into one another, so a source rooted in an element cannot run dry. Earthquakes explain why springs vanish or appear, and he disputes Theophrastus' claims that felling woods or tilling land changes the water supply. He accepts Thales' primacy of water in part but rejects his floating earth, and notes the Egyptian division of elements into male and female.","ideas":["Rain soaks only the topsoil and cannot feed great rivers","Underground air, in cold and darkness, turns into water","All elements arise from all; what returns on itself is never exhausted","In the Stoic cycle, fire is the world's end and moisture its beginning"],"terms":[{"t":"transmutation of elements","d":"The Stoic doctrine that earth, water, air and fire pass into one another, so that loss in one is kept in another."}]},{"i":551,"j":561,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, chs. 15–19","title":"The earth's veins and the dying mullet","summary":"Seneca gives his own view that the earth is built like a body, with channels for water and air like veins and arteries, and with varied fluids that harden into metals, stone or bitumen; springs flow when a vein is opened and stop when it heals or is exhausted. Wells that fill and empty at fixed intervals are compared to the regular cycles of fevers and seasons. He describes a hidden underground world of caverns, marshes and blind creatures, where fish may be dug up. Anticipating ridicule, he turns to attack the luxury of diners who watch a mullet change colour as it dies in a glass jar, while they shun the deathbeds of their own kin. He returns to show that poisonous fish thrown up from underground prove great hidden waters.","ideas":["The earth has veins of water as the body has veins of blood","Nature keeps fixed periods below ground as above","Gluttons feast their eyes on a dying fish but avoid dying friends","Underground waters are vast enough to supply rivers without end"],"terms":[]},{"i":562,"j":585,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, chs. 20–26","title":"Strange properties of waters and vanishing rivers","summary":"Seneca gives four causes for the different tastes of water: the soil it flows through, earth turned to water, air turned to water, and contamination. He explains petrifying, maddening and poisonous waters, quoting Ovid, and reports hot springs as Empedocles explains them, by underground fires, or as heated by sulphur. He lists waters that kill unnoticed, such as the Styx, rivers that change the colour of sheep, and lakes where heavy objects float, explaining floating by relative weight and describing floating islands. He doubts some medical claims as vulgar errors, explains crystal as compressed ice, gives reasons why some rivers rise in summer, and discusses rivers that sink and reappear, including the Alpheus and Arethuse. Standing waters and the sea, he ends, purge themselves of impurities.","ideas":["Water takes on the character of the ground and air it passes through","Whether a body floats depends on its weight relative to the water","Rivers that disappear run underground and burst out again","Enclosed waters and the sea throw off foreign matter"],"terms":[{"t":"krystallos (crystal)","d":"Greek term for both ice and the transparent stone believed to form from long-frozen water."}]},{"i":586,"j":596,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, chs. 27–28","title":"How the fated deluge will begin","summary":"Seneca asks how the earth will be overwhelmed when the destined flood comes, and answers that all causes will act together: rain, rivers, sea and the earth itself. Nature builds slowly but destroys suddenly, as cities taking centuries fall in an hour. Following Fabianus, he describes endless rain ruining crops, foundations sinking, torrents sweeping away towns, the Rhine and Danube overrunning their courses, and the sea advancing until only mountain tops remain, where stunned survivors pass beyond fear. He praises Ovid's lines on a sea without shore but faults him for the frivolous image of wolves swimming among sheep.","ideas":["The flood will come from all agencies acting in concert","Creation is gradual, destruction swift","A vivid picture of rain, torrents and rivers drowning the world","Ovid's grand description is spoiled by a playful detail"],"terms":[]},{"i":597,"j":609,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book III, chs. 28–30","title":"The deluge as nature's decree and renewal","summary":"Seneca considers objections that rain cannot drown the earth and that only the sea can, and grants that rain is only the prelude: the sea, driven by fate, will rise from mid-ocean above the mountains, since the earth is a sphere and the sea level with it. Flood and conflagration are both appointed means by which God ends the old order. He reports Berosus' dating of fire and flood by planetary conjunctions in Cancer and Capricorn, and applies the Stoic principle that the world, like an embryo, contains from its beginning everything it will undergo, including the flood. The earth itself will dissolve into water, all names and boundaries will vanish, and mankind will perish in a day. Afterwards the waters will recede and a new, innocent humanity will arise, though vice will soon return.","ideas":["Fire and water are lords of the earth, its origin and its grave","The world's destruction is contained in its constitution from the start","Water everywhere, above and below, makes the flood easy for nature","A renewed race begins innocent but soon falls into vice"],"terms":[{"t":"conflagration","d":"The Stoic world-ending fire which Seneca pairs with the deluge as an alternative mode of destruction."}]},{"i":610,"j":621,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book IV, Preface","title":"Lucilius in Sicily and the danger of flattery","summary":"Seneca praises Lucilius' peaceful governorship of Sicily and warns him above all against flatterers, who succeed even when rejected and most of all when open. He cites Plancus, Crispus Passienus and Demetrius on flattery's arts, and holds up his brother Gallio as a man who repelled every compliment. Rather than accept others' praise, Lucilius should commend himself truthfully, and Seneca supplies a self-review: devotion to poetry and philosophy despite poverty, loyalty to friends under Caligula and in the time of Messalina and Narcissus, refusal of bribes and of suicide as escape. Quoting Virgil, Ovid and Menander on universal vice, he urges withdrawal into oneself and offers to lead Lucilius' mind away from his province, which once saw the fall of Pompey and Lepidus.","ideas":["Flattery works best when it is detected and refused","Gallio as a model of a man impervious to praise","Honest self-assessment is better than borrowed praise","Sicily's history shows how quickly fortune casts down the great"],"terms":[]},{"i":622,"j":632,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book IV, chs. 1–2","title":"The Nile's course, cataracts and flood","summary":"Seneca takes up the deferred question of why the Nile floods in summer, first rejecting the claimed likeness of the Danube to the Nile. He describes the river gathering at Philae, the roaring Cataracts whose noise drove away settlers, and the boatmen who ride the rapids. The first rise is seen near the rock called Abaton and two crags called the veins of the Nile, where priests and magistrates make offerings; below Memphis the water is spread by canals over all Egypt. The flood both waters and fertilises the land, so that Egypt owes its very soil to the river. He adds Balbillus' report of dolphins defeating crocodiles at the Heracleotic mouth, the crocodile hunters of Tentyra, and years when the Nile failed to rise.","ideas":["The Nile floods at the driest season, giving Egypt its harvest","The Cataracts and the daring boatmen who run them","The river adds soil rather than wearing it away","Dolphins rout crocodiles by striking their soft bellies"],"terms":[{"t":"Abaton","d":"The rock near Philae, approached only by priests, where the Nile's rise is first observed."}]},{"i":633,"j":637,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book IV, ch. 2","title":"Rejected theories of the Nile flood","summary":"Seneca examines explanations of the summer flood starting with the oldest. Anaxagoras' melting snows of Ethiopia, shared by the tragic poets, fail because Ethiopia is intensely hot and snow-fed rivers swell in spring, not over four summer months. Thales' and Euthymenes' view that the Etesian winds hold back the river or drive in a fresh Atlantic sea is refuted by timing, by the muddy water and by traders' reports. Oenopides' idea of winter heat stored underground is wrong, since caves are warm in winter only because they shut out cold. Diogenes of Apollonia's theory that the sun-baked south draws water from the north leaves unexplained why other rivers do not rise and why the Nile is fresh.","ideas":["Ethiopia's heat rules out snow as the source","The flood begins before and lasts after the Etesian winds","Underground warmth in winter is only exclusion of cold","A sea-fed Nile could not be so sweet"],"terms":[{"t":"Etesian winds","d":"Seasonal summer winds that some held responsible for damming the Nile's outflow."}]},{"i":638,"j":648,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book IV, chs. 3–6","title":"How hail forms and the hail-watchers of Cleonae","summary":"After a break in the text, Seneca turns to hail, which on Posidonius' authority forms when a whole rain-charged cloud freezes, and which is round because drops tend to be globular and are worn in falling. Snow, in contrast, is loose, like hoar-frost, and forms close to the earth. He explains why hail falls in spring rather than winter by the greater variation and warmth of spring air, and reports, without endorsing it, the Stoic view that cold air from thawing northern lands turns spring rain into hail. He then mocks the Stoic tale of the official hail-watchers of Cleonae, at whose warning people offered a lamb, or blood from a pricked finger, to turn the clouds away.","ideas":["Hail is ice suspended in mid-air; snow is floating hoar-frost","Spring's changeable warmth makes larger drops and hail","Northern thaw may chill southern air into hail","Buying off hail with blood offerings is ridiculed"],"terms":[{"t":"chalazophylakes (hail-guards)","d":"Officials at Cleonae appointed to watch for hail and signal for sacrifices to avert it."}]},{"i":649,"j":661,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book IV, chs. 7–13","title":"Snow near the earth and the luxury of snow","summary":"Seneca dismisses the rational defences of the Cleonae practice and notes the Twelve Tables' laws against charming away crops. He explains that snow forms in the air nearest the earth, which is warmer because of fresh exhalations, reflected sunlight and shelter from wind, and dense air holds heat better, as Democritus' experiment with copper, glass and silver vessels shows. He refutes the claim that mountain tops should be warmer as nearer the sun, since mountains are negligible beside the universe. Imagining Lucilius' objection that such study is useless, he attacks the rich who buy and store snow and ice, paying for water and air that nature gave free, and diagnoses their craving as the fever of an overloaded stomach.","ideas":["Air near the earth is denser and warmer, suited to snow","Mountains are tiny on the scale of the universe","Luxury puts a price on water, the one thing rich and poor once shared","Habit dulls every stimulant, driving ever greater excess"],"terms":[]},{"i":662,"j":670,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book V, chs. 1–5","title":"Defining wind and its causes","summary":"Seneca defines wind as air in motion, preferably air flowing in one direction, since air, like the sea, is never wholly still, as dust in a sunbeam shows. He reports Democritus' view that wind arises when many atoms crowd a small void, and refutes it because cloudy, crowded air is often windless while breezes spring up when the sun rarefies the air. He offers several causes: the earth's exhalations, long evaporation, and the dispersal of accumulated particles by the sun, and he mentions with a joke the comparison to the world's digestion. His chief view is that air has an inherent power of motion, as water and even fire have a vital, generative power.","ideas":["Wind is air moving in one direction","Democritus' crowded-atoms theory fails against observation","Air has its own native capacity for movement","Air differs from wind as a lake from a river"],"terms":[{"t":"atoms","d":"Democritus' term for the particles whose crowding in a small void he held to cause wind."}]},{"i":671,"j":684,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book V, chs. 7–15","title":"Morning breezes, Etesians, squalls and underground winds","summary":"Seneca explains dawn breezes from rivers and valleys as night-gathered exhalations pushed out at daybreak and dispersed by the sun, strongest in spring and summer. He rejects the theory that the Etesian winds come from northern snows thawed by the sun, since these sleepy winds rise only when morning breezes have died and do not blow before the solstice. Cloud winds burst from hollow clouds, whirlwinds form when wind is deflected and spins, and a fiery whirlwind is called prester. Winds also come from caverns underground, where rivers, lakes and inflammable matter exist. Asclepiodotus' story of Philip's miners finding vast underground rivers leads Seneca to condemn the ancient greed that drove men into the earth for gold.","ideas":["Morning breezes collapse at the sight of the sun","Etesian winds do not fit the snow-thaw theory","Squalls and whirlwinds come from bursting and eddying air","Mining shows avarice is an ancient vice, not a modern one"],"terms":[{"t":"prēstēr (fire-wind)","d":"A whirlwind that spins long and violently enough to ignite."},{"t":"enkolpiai (gulf breezes)","d":"Greek name for morning breezes that flow out of bays and valleys."}]},{"i":685,"j":698,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book V, chs. 16–18","title":"The twelve winds and their misuse by man","summary":"Seneca names the four cardinal winds with Ovid's lines, then follows Varro's twelvefold scheme by sunrise and sunset at equinox and solstices, giving Greek and Latin names such as Subsolanus, Vulturnus, Favonius, Corus and Africus. The Stoics count twelve winds because the heavens are divided into twelve parts by the zones, horizon and meridian. He lists local winds, including Circius in Gaul, to which Augustus built a temple. The winds are a work of Providence, keeping air fresh, distributing rain, winnowing grain and linking nations. But men use them to sail to war, as Xerxes, Alexander and Crassus did; the fault is not God's but ours, since every gift can be turned to harm.","ideas":["Winds are classified by points of sunrise and sunset","Twelve divisions of heaven yield twelve winds","Providence gave winds to keep the world healthy and connected","Human madness turns the winds into instruments of war"],"terms":[]},{"i":699,"j":714,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 1–4","title":"The Campanian earthquake and comfort against fear","summary":"Seneca reports the winter earthquake that struck Pompeii and Campania in the consulship of Regulus and Virginius, with damage at Herculaneum, Nuceria and Naples, six hundred sheep killed and people driven mad. Since the earth itself is the one thing thought stable, earthquake seems the worst fear, without refuge. He answers that death is the same whatever its manner, that no place is exempt, and that those leaving Campania gain nothing. A tiny ailment or a drop of water can kill, so fearing only grand catastrophes is folly; it is even a comfort that the earth too is mortal. Such events are not the anger of gods but have natural causes, and knowing them dispels fear, so he resolves to revisit the subject of a youthful book.","ideas":["Earthquake seems uniquely terrible because it shakes the ground itself","All death is equal, whether by a stone or a mountain","Fear is folly when there is no escape","Knowledge of natural causes removes superstitious terror"],"terms":[]},{"i":715,"j":724,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 5–11","title":"Earthquake theories from water, fire and earth","summary":"Seneca notes that causes have been sought in water, fire, air and earth, and asks indulgence for early thinkers who first dared to look beneath nature's surface. He rejects Thales' floating earth, since the earth would then shake constantly and everywhere. Others argue that underground rivers and a hidden sea can swell, erode and batter the earth; Seneca supports their existence with the Tigris, the Alpheus, and the report of Nero's two officers who found huge marshes and two rocks pouring out water near the Nile's sources. Anaxagoras and others blame underground fire, either bursting clouds or burning away supports. Anaximenes holds that parts of the earth fall from age, and some think fire boils underground waters into shaking vapour.","ideas":["Early inquirers deserve credit even when crude","Thales' theory fails because quakes are local","An underground sea and rivers can strike the earth from below","Collapse of aged or burnt-out parts may cause shocks"],"terms":[]},{"i":725,"j":736,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 12–17","title":"Air as the cause: Archelaus to Strato","summary":"Seneca turns to those who blame air. Archelaus says winds forced into underground hollows struggle to escape, which explains the calm before a quake, as before the Campanian one. Aristotle and Theophrastus point to exhalations that rebound on themselves, and Strato to the flight of heat and cold from each other underground. Others compare the earth's water and air channels to the body's veins, with quakes like a diseased pulse, or describe air entering through pores and trapped by the sea. Seneca favours the majority view that the earth is full of vital air, which also feeds the stars, and that this most restless element, when blocked like the river Araxes, bursts out violently, as shown by winds issuing from chasms after the quake at Chalcis.","ideas":["Stillness before a quake shows the wind is held below","Heat and cold cannot share a place and drive each other out","The earth holds abundant vital breath","Blocked air, like a dammed river, gains force from resistance"],"terms":[{"t":"pneuma / spiritus (air, breath)","d":"The mobile, vital air within the earth that Seneca treats as the chief cause of earthquakes."}]},{"i":737,"j":745,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 18–21","title":"Air confined; Metrodorus, Democritus, Epicurus","summary":"Seneca concludes that air is the chief cause: at rest it is harmless, but when compressed and cut off it batters its barriers until it escapes, and no prison can hold wind, since enclosed air is no longer wind. Bodily trembling likewise comes from disturbed internal air. He reports Metrodorus' comparison of a quake to a voice resounding in a barrel, Democritus' combination of water and air, and Epicurus' many possible causes, though Epicurus too ranks air first. The Stoics agree that air is the strongest force, able to raise mountains and islands such as Thera, Therasia and a new island in the Aegean.","ideas":["Wind cannot be imprisoned; it asserts its freedom","Trembling in bodies and earth comes from obstructed air","Epicurus refuses to fix on a single cause","Air raises new islands from the sea"],"terms":[]},{"i":746,"j":757,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 21–26","title":"Kinds of quake and the caverns beneath","summary":"Posidonius distinguishes a vertical shaking and a sideways tilting; Seneca adds a third, trembling, which does least harm. Shaking may come from falling underground rock, as Asclepiodotus describes, and tilting from trapped air, a view held by Callisthenes, whose killing Seneca calls an eternal blot on Alexander. Callisthenes explains quakes near the sea, and Poseidon's title Earthshaker, by air entering under the sea. Seneca argues that air works in deep caverns, not near the surface, as swallowed cities like Atalanta and Sidon show, and that quakes are confined to the extent of such caverns, never two hundred miles. He disputes claims that Egypt and Delos never quake, and Callisthenes' view that coasts and islands are safer, citing Pompeii, Herculaneum, Paphos and Tyre.","ideas":["Shaking, tilting and trembling are distinct movements","Alexander's guilt in killing Callisthenes outweighs his conquests","Quakes arise deep down and spread only as far as the caverns","Coasts are especially, not less, subject to earthquakes"],"terms":[{"t":"Enosichthon (Earthshaker)","d":"Greek title of Neptune, explained by the frequency of quakes near the sea."}]},{"i":758,"j":778,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VI, chs. 27–32","title":"Campanian aftermath and contempt of death","summary":"Seneca explains the Campanian oddities: the sheep died from poisonous air released from the stagnant depths, to which grazing animals are most exposed; people went mad from terror; a statue split because air was trapped in hollow bronze, as whole regions like Sicily and Spain were torn away by such forces. Aftershocks continued because some air remained, and a philosopher in a bath saw floor tiles part and close. Turning to consolation, he says that study of nature gives courage: life is a trifle, contempt of life a great thing, and whoever despises it can face any upheaval unmoved. Since earth itself will perish, we should be ready to return life, make death an intimate friend, and not dread its name.","ideas":["Pestilential air from the depths killed the flocks","Fear unhinges weak minds in public disaster","Leftover air explains prolonged aftershocks","Readiness to die frees us from fear of every catastrophe"],"terms":[]},{"i":779,"j":787,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (1 of 6)","title":"Why comets matter; early theories and Epigenes","summary":"Seneca opens by observing that people ignore the regular wonders of the heavens and gape only at the unusual, such as eclipses and comets. He calls the study of the stars the most exalted of inquiries and asks whether stars are pure fire or earthy bodies carried through fire, and whether the earth or the heavens revolve. Comets cannot yet be understood because records of them are too few; he surveys Democritus, Eudoxus, Conon, and the conflicting reports of Epigenes and Apollonius of Myndus on Chaldaean views. He then sets out Epigenes' theory that Saturn's influence and whirlwinds produce lightning, beams and torches, and begins refuting it: whirlwinds stay low and brief, while beams shine high and long, as in the comet that preceded the destruction of Helice and Buris.","ideas":["Men admire what is strange rather than what is great","Knowledge of comets requires a long record of past appearances","Whirlwinds stay below the clouds and cannot explain high, lasting lights"],"terms":[{"t":"trabs (beam meteor)","d":"A long fiery light that stays shining in one part of the sky, distinguished from the moving torch."},{"t":"Chaldaeans","d":"Babylonian astrologers whose supposed teaching on comets Epigenes and Apollonius report differently."}]},{"i":788,"j":794,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (2 of 6)","title":"Epigenes' whirlwind theory of comets refuted","summary":"Epigenes divides comets into stationary ones with hair on all sides, formed low in thick air from earth's exhalations, and moving ones that pass among the stars. Both, he holds, are fires kindled by winds or whirlwinds. Seneca answers point by point: comets appear in still air, do not grow or fall with the wind, move east and west in curved paths no wind could give, and travel far slower and more regularly than any whirlwind. Whirlwinds are short-lived, cannot rise above the clouds, and would be shattered by the revolution of the heavens; nor could a round, pillar-like eddy produce the scattered trail of a comet.","ideas":["A comet's slow, regular course is unlike the violent, erratic motion of wind","No whirlwind lasts a day, while comets last up to six months","The ordered motion of the heavens leaves no room for storms among the stars"],"terms":[{"t":"exhalations","d":"Dry and moist vapours rising from the earth, which Epigenes takes as the fuel of comets."}]},{"i":795,"j":802,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (3 of 6)","title":"Planetary conjunction, Artemidorus and Ephorus rejected","summary":"Seneca notes that comets appear in all parts of the sky and in varied shapes, and turns to the old view that a comet is the blended light of two planets in conjunction. He objects that planets and comets are seen together, that conjunctions happen yearly without comets and pass quickly, that the planets are vastly far apart, and that comets range outside the zodiac. Artemidorus' claim of countless unseen wandering stars, and his solid vault of heaven with windows letting in outer fire, is dismissed as a tissue of lies. Huge comets recorded after Demetrius of Syria and under Attalus could not be made of combined stars, and the historian Ephorus, who said the Helice comet split in two, is discredited as a teller of marvels.","ideas":["Comets outlast any meeting of planets","Artemidorus' solid sky could not stay aloft","Historians often add falsehoods to please readers"],"terms":[]},{"i":803,"j":814,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (4 of 6)","title":"Apollonius, Zeno, and the Stoic view of comets","summary":"Apollonius of Myndus holds that comets are true heavenly bodies with hidden orbits, appearing only at the low point of their course, and varied in size and colour. Seneca reports the replies: comets often reach full size at once, stray outside the zodiac, and can be seen through like a cloud. Zeno thought comets arise from stars uniting their rays; others call them real stars or mere passing fires. Most Stoics treat them, like beams, torches and other fiery portents recorded by Posidonius, as fires formed in dense air that wander where their fuel leads, citing the six-month comet of Nero's reign, which moved opposite to Claudius' comet.","ideas":["Comets may be hidden by the sun's rays, as one seen during an eclipse","Most Stoics: comets feed like fires and follow their fuel","The comets of Claudius and Nero took different paths"],"terms":[{"t":"Posidonius","d":"Stoic writer cited for records of flaming pillars, shields and other sky portents."}]},{"i":815,"j":823,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (5 of 6)","title":"Seneca's own view: comets are permanent bodies","summary":"Breaking with his school, Seneca argues that a comet is one of nature's permanent works, not a sudden fire: the air is too changeable to hold a lasting fire, comets never sink toward the earth, and their curved, steady course belongs to stars. He denies that all moving stars must keep to the zodiac and thinks the universe likely has many paths. That comets' laws are unknown is no surprise, since even the mind cannot explain itself and knowledge of the planets is recent; later ages will find out much that is hidden. Answering Apollonius, he says comets are round bodies whose light streams out, and that nature delights in variety, the whole universe being a harmony of discords.","ideas":["Fires born of the atmosphere are brief; comets are lasting","Future generations will wonder at our ignorance","Nature does not work to a single pattern"],"terms":[{"t":"concordia discors (harmony of discords)","d":"Seneca's phrase for a universe made up of opposing elements held in order."}]},{"i":824,"j":835,"loc":"Natural Questions, Book VII (Comets) (6 of 6)","title":"Comets as signs, reverence, and the neglect of philosophy","summary":"Seneca grants that comets foretell events, but as signs set within the order of the universe and affecting a whole year, not as weather signs. He rejects Aristotle's claims that comets move slowly because they are heavy and keep near the poles, citing the paths of the recent comets, and admits that on such matters we can only conjecture. Recalling Aristotle's call for reverence in speaking of the gods, he says God himself is hidden and perceived only by thought, and that nature, like Eleusis, reveals her mysteries gradually over the ages. He ends by lamenting that Romans pursue luxury and effeminacy and keep the lines of actors alive while the philosophical schools die out for want of pupils.","ideas":["Comets are inwoven in the universe, not accidental fires","God, the greater part of his work, is unseen","Vice advances while wisdom finds no students"],"terms":[{"t":"Sextii","d":"A recent Roman philosophical sect that Seneca says died out at its start."}]},{"i":836,"j":872,"loc":"On Benefits, front matter","title":"Introduction, translator's preface and contents","summary":"An editorial note introduces De Beneficiis as a seven-book moral essay of about 63 AD on giving and receiving benefits. The translator, Aubrey Stewart, writing in 1887, recalls Seneca's standing among the Church Fathers and in the Middle Ages, earlier English translations, and possible echoes in Shakespeare. He quotes at length Dean Merivale's assessment, which compares Seneca with St. Paul as a moral reformer, rejects their supposed correspondence as forgery, and weighs Seneca's ideals against his wealth and political compromises. A detailed table of contents then lists the topics of all seven books.","ideas":["Seneca represents the moral tone of Roman society under the Caesars","Merivale sees Seneca and Paul as parallel but independent reformers","The contents outline each book's questions and examples"],"terms":[]},{"i":873,"j":881,"loc":"On Benefits, Book I (1 of 3)","title":"Ingratitude, careless giving, and the three Graces","summary":"Dedicating the work to Aebutius Liberalis, Seneca names ignorance of how to give and receive benefits as one of society's worst faults. Givers create ingratitude by choosing unworthy recipients, giving late, grudgingly or insultingly, and demanding returns; yet, like the gods, we should keep giving despite the ungrateful, since a benefit is expenditure, not an investment. He interprets the three Graces as the circle of giving, receiving and returning, then mocks Chrysippus and the poets for burying the subject in fables about their names. The real aim, he says, is to teach men to give, receive and return willingly, and to strive to outdo benefactors.","ideas":["A benefit is received in the spirit in which it is given","He who does not give sins earlier than the ungrateful","The Graces' circling dance shows benefits passing back to the giver"],"terms":[{"t":"beneficium (benefit)","d":"A kindness freely given, the central subject of the treatise."},{"t":"the Graces","d":"Aglaia, Euphrosyne and Thalia, read here as an allegory of the exchange of benefits."}]},{"i":882,"j":891,"loc":"On Benefits, Book I (2 of 3)","title":"A benefit lies in the mind, not the gift","summary":"Seneca argues that money, offices and other gifts are only signs of a benefit; the benefit itself is the goodwill of the giver, which no loss or violence can undo. Since the thing given is neither good nor bad, a willing small gift outweighs a grudging large one, as worship lies in the worshipper's piety, not the victim. The poor pupil Aeschines, giving himself to Socrates, outdid the rich. After a gap in the text, Seneca denounces open adultery and plunder in Rome, then reflects that vice merely shifts its forms in every age. Ingratitude is the worst crime, but should be guarded against in oneself and pardoned in others; one should sometimes help even the ungrateful.","ideas":["The visible gift is only the trace of a benefit","We prize a willing hand over a full one","Vices shift like tides but never disappear"],"terms":[{"t":"voluntas (goodwill)","d":"The intention of the giver, in which Seneca locates the benefit itself."}]},{"i":892,"j":897,"loc":"On Benefits, Book I (3 of 3)","title":"What to give and how to choose gifts","summary":"Seneca ranks gifts as necessary, useful and pleasant: first rescue from danger, then things without which life is not worth living, such as liberty and a good conscience, then family and home; next money and office in moderation. Gifts should suit time, place and receiver, avoid insulting allusions, and preferably last, so they keep the giver in mind; rare things please more than costly ones. The Corinthians' grant of citizenship flattered Alexander, whom Seneca condemns as a brigand compared with Hercules. Indiscriminate giving earns no thanks, so even shared gifts should carry a mark of special favour, and generosity must be ruled by discretion.","ideas":["Necessary gifts come before useful and pleasant ones","Lasting gifts keep the memory of the giver alive","Generosity without judgment turns into extravagance"],"terms":[]},{"i":898,"j":915,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (1 of 6)","title":"Giving promptly, graciously and without being asked","summary":"Opening a new stage of the subject, Seneca's rule is to give as we would wish to receive: willingly, quickly and without hesitation. The best gift anticipates the request, since asking is shameful and nothing costs more than what is bought by prayers; delay, sullen manner and middlemen wear away gratitude, and long uncertainty is a kind of torture. Kind words should accompany kind acts, and reproaches be saved for another time. Tiberius' payment of Marius Nepos' debts with insulting rebukes, and his later practice of making debtors explain themselves to the senate, are given as reprimands rather than benefits. Finally, honours should be given in public, help in want or disgrace in private.","ideas":["Hesitation is next to refusal","Manner of giving decides the value of the gift","A gift that shames the receiver is no benefit"],"terms":[]},{"i":916,"j":919,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (2 of 6)","title":"Secret giving, silence, and the pride of givers","summary":"Seneca praises Arcesilaus, who slipped money under a proud, poor friend's pillow, and argues that the giver should be content with his own conscience rather than demand to be known. The giver should forget the gift and the receiver remember it; constant reminders make the rescued wish themselves unsaved, and the story should be told by the receiver. Benefits, like crops and children, must be tended and accompanied by love. As an example of arrogance he describes Gaius Caesar offering his slippered left foot to be kissed by the pardoned Pompeius Pennus, and he ends with an address to pride, which turns gifts into outrages.","ideas":["Benefits must be fostered after they are given","Boasting of a gift is asking for its return","A gift given with haughtiness earns dislike"],"terms":[]},{"i":920,"j":923,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (3 of 6)","title":"Harmful gifts, proportion, and the game of ball","summary":"Seneca says we must refuse harmful gifts however hard we are begged, looking to what the receiver will later be glad to have received. A gift should suit the means of the giver and the standing of the receiver, neither too large nor too small. He calls Alexander's gift of a city, and his reply that he considered only what suited himself to give, foolish. He defends Antigonus for denying a cynic both a talent and a penny, since a professed despiser of money should not ask for it. Using Chrysippus' simile of a ball game, he urges givers to throw gently to novices, help others return their gifts, and welcome repayment.","ideas":["Granting what harms is hatred in the mask of kindness","Virtue sets the measure of what to give","Giver and receiver must cooperate like two ball players"],"terms":[]},{"i":924,"j":935,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (4 of 6)","title":"From whom to accept, and how to receive","summary":"Turning to the receiver, Seneca says we should choose our benefactors more carefully than our creditors, since a benefit binds us to lasting friendship. Gifts taken under compulsion from tyrants or pirates create no obligation, and benefits done by chance or without goodwill, such as a lion sparing its old keeper, are not benefits. Brutus was right to accept life from Caesar without owing him a son's gratitude; a captive may take ransom from a vile man but treat it as a loan. Julius Graecinus refused gifts from Fabius Persicus and Rebilius. Benefits should be accepted cheerfully, openly and with fitting thanks, as Furnius thanked Augustus, and the receiver should watch, as Chrysippus says, for a chance to repay.","ideas":["No one owes what he could not refuse","Receiving gratefully pays the first instalment","Secret thanks are a way of denying the debt"],"terms":[]},{"i":936,"j":943,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (5 of 6)","title":"Causes of ingratitude: self-love, greed, jealousy","summary":"Seneca names three causes of ingratitude: excessive self-esteem, greed and jealousy. The self-admiring man thinks he earned what he got and was undervalued, like the rich augur Cnaeus Lentulus, who complained that Augustus had cost him his studies. Greed and ambition always look forward to the next prize, and jealousy breeds endless comparison, whereas a fair mind makes the most of what it has received. Even some philosophers complain that nature gave other animals better gifts, though the gods gave humans reason and lordship over the earth. Whoever scorns the gods' gifts will be grateful to no one, and gratitude to the gods costs nothing.","ideas":["The ambitious man never looks back at where he started","Spite can find fault with any benefit","Humans are nature's favoured children"],"terms":[]},{"i":944,"j":948,"loc":"On Benefits, Book II (6 of 6)","title":"The Stoic paradox: receiving gratefully repays","summary":"Seneca takes up the Stoic paradox that whoever receives a benefit with goodwill has returned it. Since the giver aimed only to help and please, his purpose is achieved when the gift is well received; the material return is a further addition. Answering the ball-game objection, he says the game, not the player, is left incomplete. Phidias' statue shows that the work, the fame and the profit are separate fruits. Because language uses one word for several things, a benefit is both an act repaid by goodwill and a thing that still calls for a material return, just as other Stoic paradoxes coexist with ordinary practice.","ideas":["The intention is enough to discharge the debt of goodwill","Gratitude owed and recompense owed are distinct","Words like brave and economical cover distinct things"],"terms":[{"t":"paradox (Stoic)","d":"A Stoic claim contrary to common opinion, such as that a wise man cannot be injured."}]},{"i":949,"j":957,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (1 of 6)","title":"Forgetting benefits, and whether to punish ingratitude by law","summary":"Seneca says the worst ingrate is the one who forgets a benefit, since remembrance costs nothing and forgetting shows it was never valued. Benefits fade because we fix our minds on new desires and on the future, a fault for which he credits Epicurus' complaint that we do not treasure past goods. He then asks whether ingratitude should be actionable at law, as in the schools. Only Macedonia has such a law; benefits lose their nobility if they can be sued for, and gratitude loses its merit if forced. No judge could weigh motives and circumstances, and a benefit's value differs with the giver's wealth, timing and manner.","ideas":["Memory makes men grateful; hope makes them forget","There is no credit in gratitude unless ingratitude is safe","A benefit's worth cannot be fixed by a court"],"terms":[]},{"i":958,"j":966,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (2 of 6)","title":"Further arguments against laws on ingratitude","summary":"Seneca continues: many unshowy services, such as good counsel or comfort in grief, outweigh coveted gifts, and no day is set for repayment, no witnesses attend the greatest benefits, and no penalty could fit gifts of life. Parents receive special legal authority because rearing children is a gamble, unlike other benefits. Legal enforcement would make men less willing to receive and give, turn benefits into commerce, and reveal how common ingratitude is, removing its shame, as has happened with divorce and adultery. The ingrate is already punished by universal hatred, fear and the loss of the joy that the grateful man enjoys.","ideas":["Seals and witnesses show human distrust","The giver acts like a god, the demander like a money-lender","Hate and inner torment are punishment enough"],"terms":[]},{"i":967,"j":973,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (3 of 6)","title":"Can a slave benefit his master?","summary":"Seneca addresses the question raised by Hecaton and others, who distinguish benefits from strangers, duties from relatives and services from slaves. He argues that virtue is open to slave, freedman and king alike, and that if slaves could not confer benefits, subjects and soldiers could not either. A slave who dies for his master or keeps his secrets under torture gives a benefit made greater by his condition. Slavery binds only the body; the mind stays free. Whatever a slave does beyond his required duties, of his own will, is a benefit, as Chrysippus' definition of a slave as a lifelong hireling implies.","ideas":["The question is the giver's intention, not his rank","The mind of a slave cannot be bought or sold","Service beyond duty becomes a benefit"],"terms":[{"t":"officium / ministerium","d":"Duty owed by relatives and service owed by slaves, distinguished from a benefit freely given."}]},{"i":974,"j":982,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (4 of 6)","title":"Examples of slaves' benefits; common human origin","summary":"Seneca gives examples: slaves at Grumentum who pretended to lead their mistress to punishment in order to save her, Vettius' slave who killed his master and himself to spare him capture, Domitius' physician-slave who gave a harmless drug instead of poison, a slave who died in place of his proscribed master, a slave who foiled the informer Maro under Tiberius, and Rufus' slave who saved him from Augustus' anger. He concludes that all people descend from the same source, the universe, so pedigree is no ground for pride. Those who despise slaves are themselves slaves to lust and fawn on others' servants. This prepares the question of whether sons can outdo their fathers in benefits.","ideas":["Slaves have given masters both life and death","No one is better born except by a nobler disposition","The proud at home are the most servile abroad"],"terms":[]},{"i":983,"j":986,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (5 of 6)","title":"Can sons give fathers more than they received?","summary":"Seneca rejects the argument that a son can never outdo his father because the son owes his very power of giving to him. Things often outgrow their origins, as rivers, trees and buildings outgrow springs, roots and foundations, and we do not rank nurses or remote ancestors above all later achievements. Birth itself is a small and doubtful gift shared with beasts and worms; the son who saves his father gives life to one who knows and values it. To live is not the good, but to live well, and a son who leads an upright life returns more than he received. Sons such as Xenophon, Plato, Socrates, Agrippa and Augustus saved their fathers' names from oblivion.","ideas":["A thing can be greater than that from which it began","Mere life is shared with flies and worms","Saving a life is a greater gift than giving it unknowingly"],"terms":[]},{"i":987,"j":992,"loc":"On Benefits, Book III (6 of 6)","title":"Sons who conquered their fathers in benefits","summary":"Seneca cites young Scipio, who saved his father in battle and whose later glory made his father known as 'the father of Scipio'. Teachers too are surpassed by pupils without being wronged. He then offers his own arguments: whatever is better than life can outdo the gift of life, and a father saved more than once receives more than he gave. This contest does not weaken filial respect but spurs it, and Seneca urges youths to compete. Aeneas, the Sicilian brothers at Aetna, Antigonus and Manlius are given as sons who outdid their fathers, and he calls both victor and vanquished happy.","ideas":["The beginning is not equal to the thing at its greatest","Virtue is ambitious to outstrip those ahead","Being outdone by one's son is a happy defeat"],"terms":[]},{"i":993,"j":1012,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (1 of 5)","title":"Benefits sought for themselves; God as giver","summary":"Seneca poses the central question: are giving and returning benefits desirable in themselves? Virtue is its own reward, and he attacks the Epicureans for making virtue serve pleasure. If benefits were given for profit, the rich and the gods would never give; Epicurus' answer that God ignores the world is refuted by universal prayer and by the abundance nature provides: land, waters, metals, food, the arts and the body's growth. Those who credit nature instead of God merely change the name, since nature, fate and fortune are names of one God, called by the Stoics Jupiter, Liber, Hercules and Mercurius.","ideas":["The reward of honourable action lies in the act","The gods give without hope of return","Nature and God are one, differing only in function"],"terms":[{"t":"honestum (the honourable)","d":"What is right and to be sought for its own sake, apart from profit."},{"t":"fatum (fate)","d":"A connected chain of causes, whose first cause is God."}]},{"i":1013,"j":1023,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (2 of 5)","title":"Choosing recipients without seeking returns","summary":"Seneca replies to the objection that choosing recipients carefully shows self-interest: honourable acts are done for their own sake, but reason still decides to whom, when and how, as with returning a deposit. He chooses an honest, grateful person, rich or poor, not one likely to repay, and points to help given to departing strangers and to the care taken over wills as giving without hope of return. Calling a benefit a loan is only a figure, and the only return is the consciousness of a good act, as with the sun's service to us. He contrasts Epicurean idleness with Stoic labour for others, says giving in order to get is a broker's language, and adds that benefactors love to keep giving.","ideas":["Choice of recipient is part of acting rightly","A benefit is like a loan that cannot be repaid","We nurse even failed benefits out of love of giving"],"terms":[]},{"i":1024,"j":1030,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (3 of 5)","title":"Gratitude is desirable for its own sake","summary":"Against opponents who are grateful only because it pays, Seneca argues that gratitude is honourable in itself: everyone loathes the ingrate as base, not merely imprudent, and even criminals wish to seem beneficent. Ingratitude destroys the union that, with reason, makes weak humans masters of the earth. He mocks Epicurus' remote, powerless god, whom Epicureans still claim to worship for his majesty, which concedes that something is sought for itself. Gratitude may bring advantages but would be chosen even at a loss; whoever hopes while he repays is a legacy-hunter. The truly grateful may be unable to repay or be slandered, yet a good conscience suffices even under torture.","ideas":["Even the wicked approve the good","Reason and union are God's two gifts to man","Gratitude lies in the intention, like a pilot's skill on land"],"terms":[]},{"i":1031,"j":1037,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (4 of 5)","title":"Imitating the gods; benefits to the ungrateful","summary":"Seneca notes that the dying wish to leave life grateful though they can expect nothing, which shows the power of honour. Virtues bring advantages but lead them; the sun, moon and stars would deserve contemplation even if useless, and gratitude likewise should not be loved for profit. We aim to live by the scheme of the universe and imitate the gods, who give without reward. To the objection that the gods benefit the ungrateful too, he distinguishes two kinds of ingrate: every fool, who has all vices at least latently, and the man naturally prone to ingratitude. The wise man will benefit the first but not the second, just as no one would entrust a daughter, estate or ward to a proven abuser.","ideas":["Virtue will not admit a covetous lover","The gods act for the act's sake alone","Every fool has the seeds of every vice, but not all equally"],"terms":[{"t":"stultus (fool)","d":"In Stoic usage, anyone not wise, who therefore possesses all vices in some degree."}]},{"i":1038,"j":1043,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (5 of 5)","title":"Common gifts, trifles, and honour to ancestors","summary":"Seneca answers the objection that the gods give even to the ungrateful: some gifts, like sun, rain and winds, cannot be given to the good without reaching the bad, just as a citizen's corn dole goes to thief and honest man alike. He then distinguishes a true benefit from a trivial service such as showing the way or giving water, which is given to humanity rather than to a chosen person. He admits he will sometimes give to the unworthy for the sake of their worthy ancestors or descendants, citing consuls made for their fathers' names. Defending providence to Liberalis, he suggests the gods likewise raise men like Arrhidaeus or Caius Caesar to repay debts owed to their forebears.","ideas":["Not shutting a man out differs from choosing him","A benefit needs real value and a deliberate choice of the receiver","Gifts to the unworthy may repay debts owed to their ancestors","Providence keeps an account across generations"],"terms":[{"t":"beneficium (benefit)","d":"A useful service given willingly, for the receiver's sake, to someone judged worthy of it."},{"t":"providentia (Providence)","d":"The divine ordering that, Seneca suggests, rewards the virtues of ancestors in their descendants."}]},{"i":1044,"j":1051,"loc":"On Benefits, Book IV (end)","title":"Uncertain judgment, changed circumstances, broken promises","summary":"To the objection that one cannot know who will be grateful, Seneca replies that all action, from sowing to marriage, follows probability rather than certain truth. The wise man never regrets his plans because he makes them with an implied reservation that nothing intervene; a promise binds only while circumstances stay the same, and discovering that the receiver is ungrateful is a decisive change. Small promises may still be kept as a penalty for rashness, large ones withdrawn. Philip of Macedon's branding of a soldier who seized his rescuer's estate shows that a wrong grant should be revoked, and Zeno's small loan is explained away. The book closes on returning benefits: the grateful will remains even when poverty or a friend's wealth prevents repayment, and over-eagerness to repay is a debtor's attitude.","ideas":["We act on the appearance of truth, not certainty","Every promise carries an implied exception","Philip's soldier: ingratitude justifies revoking a gift","Too much haste to repay shows unwillingness to owe"],"terms":[{"t":"exceptio (saving clause)","d":"The tacit condition 'if nothing intervenes' with which the wise man begins every undertaking."}]},{"i":1052,"j":1058,"loc":"On Benefits, Book V (1 of 4)","title":"Can one be beaten in benefits?","summary":"Opening Book V, Seneca says the core subject is finished and he will now treat adjacent questions, praising Liberalis's generosity. He examines the saying that it is disgraceful to be outdone in benefits: no one is beaten in an honourable contest who keeps the will to win, as the Spartans kept their youth from contests decided by surrender, and the Fabii and Regulus were slain or captured, not conquered. Gratitude is measured by intention, so one is not disgraced by receiving more from kings, parents, or men like Socrates and Diogenes, who could refuse more than Alexander could give. Socrates' refusal of Archelaus's invitation is read as a joking evasion: he could have repaid the king by teaching him, for instance, the true cause of a solar eclipse, but would not freely become a slave.","ideas":["Intention, not the size of gifts, decides the contest","The mind remains unconquered when all else is lost","Diogenes outdid Alexander by needing nothing","Socrates declined Archelaus to keep his freedom"],"terms":[{"t":"voluntas (will, intention)","d":"The goodwill by which gratitude is measured, independent of fortune and means."}]},{"i":1059,"j":1064,"loc":"On Benefits, Book V (2 of 4)","title":"Can a man benefit himself?","summary":"Seneca takes up a question some Stoics debate: since we speak of being angry with or punishing ourselves, can we also benefit ourselves and owe ourselves gratitude? He sets out the case for it, then rejects it. Owing requires a creditor and debtor, as a husband requires a wife; one who gives to himself receives in the same act, like a ball whose parts return to the same place. What we do for ourselves is natural self-love, not generosity; a benefit is voluntary and given for another's sake. Words like 'brother', 'equal' and 'fidelity' need a second person, and so do giving and returning, which involve outlay and friendship.","ideas":["Debt and benefit require two persons","Self-care is instinct, not beneficence","Giving to oneself collapses giving and receiving into one act","Thanking oneself is really self-praise"],"terms":[]},{"i":1065,"j":1077,"loc":"On Benefits, Book V (3 of 4)","title":"Is no one, or everyone, ungrateful?","summary":"Seneca enjoys a set of knotty puzzles as exercise for the mind. An opponent argues from Stoic premises that a bad man cannot receive a benefit and a good man always repays, so no one is ungrateful. Seneca grants that true good cannot reach the bad, but goods of body and fortune resemble benefits, and failing to repay them is still ingratitude; Cleanthes adds that such a man would not have repaid a real benefit. The converse claim, that all men are ungrateful since all fools have every vice, Seneca accepts, citing a poet's lament and Roman examples from Coriolanus and Catilina to Marius, Sulla, Pompeius, the unnamed Caesar and Antonius, as well as the state's ingratitude to Camillus, Scipio, Cicero and Cato. He ends by calling it ingratitude not to be content with one's span of life.","ideas":["Quasi-benefits create real obligations","All fools have all vices, so all are ungrateful","Roman civil-war leaders as public ingrates","Being content with the life given is gratitude"],"terms":[{"t":"honestum (the honourable)","d":"For the Stoics the only true good, which cannot dwell in a bad man."}]},{"i":1078,"j":1087,"loc":"On Benefits, Book V (4 of 4)","title":"Benefits to relatives and against one's will","summary":"In a short dialogue with an adversary, Seneca asks whether saving a man's son benefits the father. The adversary argues that the son alone receives the benefit, since the giver may not know or may even hate the father; the father may choose to feel indebted. Speaking as a lawyer, Seneca holds that the giver's intention decides who receives, otherwise the chain of obligation would run endlessly through kin, friends and country. He adds that saving a man's hated brother still benefits him, as harsh cures do, while killing that brother would not, however glad he is. Burying a stranger's father is a benefit to the son only if done knowingly for his sake.","ideas":["The giver's intention fixes the receiver","Benefits do not spread down the family tree","A benefit can be received against one's will","Generic humanity creates no obligation"],"terms":[]},{"i":1088,"j":1095,"loc":"On Benefits, Book V (end)","title":"Reminding the forgetful of benefits","summary":"Against those who say repayment should never be asked, Seneca holds that real need for children, country or liberty may justify asking, and that unwritten social custom binds more than law. He would pass over the hopelessly ungrateful but gently remind the sluggish, whose sense of honour is only asleep, privately and without reproach. Caesar's veteran, who recalled carrying water in his helmet in Spain, shows a fair reminder rewarded; Tiberius, who answered 'I do not remember what I was', shows a man who chose to forget. Even the gods are reminded in prayer, and a docile mind, like a scholar prompted with a word, can be brought to its duty.","ideas":["Custom is stronger than law in matters of honour","Dormant gratitude may be roused by a gentle hint","Caesar's veteran and the split helmet","Being willing to take advice is the second-best virtue"],"terms":[]},{"i":1096,"j":1104,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (1 of 6)","title":"Can a benefit be taken away?","summary":"Book VI opens with Seneca offering Liberalis questions partly for exercise and partly useful, to be pursued or dropped as his face directs. A benefit, he argues, is an incorporeal act, not the thing given: the thing may be seized, but the giving cannot be undone, as nature cannot unmake past life or sight. A line from Rabirius, Antonius saying he still possesses what he gave, leads to an exhortation that wealth becomes truly one's own only when given away. A later wrong does not remove a benefit but can cancel the gratitude owed, as a judge strikes a balance between claims; Seneca weighs benefit and injury together in his own court rather than keeping two accounts.","ideas":["A benefit is an act, not its material","What you give away you keep for ever","A later injury cancels gratitude, not the benefit","Benefits and wrongs are judged in one court"],"terms":[]},{"i":1105,"j":1113,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (2 of 6)","title":"Unwilling and unknowing benefactors","summary":"Seneca asks whether we owe anything to one who helps us without meaning to. A benefit must come from the thought of a well-wisher, so we owe nothing to rivers, winds, horses or trees, nor to a man who helps by chance or under compulsion. Cures by accident are not remedies, and an enemy whose blundering lawsuit or perjury saves us has done us a service but no benefit; intention distinguishes friend from enemy. Cleanthes' tale of two slaves sent for Plato shows that the diligent searcher deserves praise and the lucky idler punishment. A benefit requires both goodwill and the thing done; goodwill without the gift earns friendship, not a debt.","ideas":["No gratitude is owed to things without will","A benefit is judged by aim, not result","Cleanthes' two slaves seeking Plato","A benefit joins goodwill and its material"],"terms":[]},{"i":1114,"j":1123,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (3 of 6)","title":"Self-interest, sale, and public gifts","summary":"One who helps only for his own profit, like a farmer feeding his flock, gives no benefit; Cleanthes separates benefits from trade. But help that serves both giver and receiver earns gratitude, and a man ransomed for another's political advantage owes thanks for being chosen, little if chosen by lot. What is bought, like corn, is not owed beyond its price. Physicians and teachers are paid for their time, but we owe them more when they become friends, giving personal care beyond their trade. Plato's ferryman, who carried everyone free, earned no special debt; a grant of citizenship to all the Gauls obliges each Gaul only as part of his nation.","ideas":["Benefits differ from trade","Being chosen is what creates the debt","Doctors and teachers earn more by personal care","Gifts to a whole people oblige each only in part"],"terms":[]},{"i":1124,"j":1130,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (4 of 6)","title":"The sun, the gods, and necessity","summary":"The adversary objects that we owe nothing to the sun and moon, which do not move for us alone and cannot stop. Seneca replies that they move for the universe, of which he is part, and gain nothing from us. Being unable to change one's will is the strongest proof of willing, as a good man cannot fail to act well; the gods are bound only by their own unchanging decision. He paints the chaos if the heavens left their posts, and argues that nature planned man deliberately, giving him rule over land and sea and a mind that seeks the gods. Like parents who force useful things on crying infants, the gods benefit us even when we disown them.","ideas":["Fixed will is greater, not lesser, will","The heavens serve us on their way to greater ends","Man is a designed, not chance, work of nature","Benefits often come unknown or against our will"],"terms":[{"t":"natura (Nature)","d":"The ordering power that, Seneca says, conceived of man before forming him."}]},{"i":1131,"j":1138,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (5 of 6)","title":"Praying for a benefactor's misfortune","summary":"Seneca condemns the over-grateful who pray that their benefactor may fall into danger so that they may rescue him, likening them to frantic lovers, a pilot praying for storms, or a general wishing his camp overrun. Wishing harm in order to cure is wicked; the danger is certain and comes first, the rescue uncertain, and the wish really aims at escaping the obligation and humbling the benefactor. He offers a better prayer that the benefactor stay prosperous and always able to give. Even the rich can be repaid by honest advice, company, and keeping secrets; above all the powerful need someone who will tell them the truth amid flattery.","ideas":["Wishing distress to repay is disguised ingratitude","Such prayers give the gods the cruel part","Pray that the benefactor never need you","The great lack truthful friends"],"terms":[]},{"i":1139,"j":1143,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (6 of 6)","title":"Demaratus, Augustus, and true friends","summary":"While Xerxes' courtiers flatter him before invading Greece, Demaratus alone warns that an unwieldy host has weight without strength and that three hundred Spartans will hold him at a pass; after Thermopylae Xerxes rewards him. Augustus, having publicised his daughter's disgrace, lamented that Agrippa or Maecenas were dead, though Seneca doubts they would have spoken frankly. Returning to his point, Seneca says one can repay the prosperous by useful advice and by teaching them that fortune is fleeting. A true friend is a rare gift; the crowds at great men's levees, divided into ranks since Gaius Gracchus and Livius Drusus, contain no friends.","ideas":["Demaratus told Xerxes the truth alone","Augustus could replace legions but not friends","Graded receptions breed no friendship","Friends are held in the heart, not the hall"],"terms":[{"t":"nomenclator","d":"A slave who told his master the names of people met, used here to mock lists of so-called friends."}]},{"i":1144,"j":1154,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VI (end)","title":"Living calmly under an obligation","summary":"Seneca presses the point that wishing trouble on a friend in order to help him prefers oneself to him and treats gratitude as a burden. Aeneas, the Sicilian brothers, Scipio or the Decii would deserve no credit had they prayed for the disasters they met. Callistratus and Rutilius refused to return from exile at the cost of public ruin. Demades' prosecution of a funeral furnisher raises whether evil wishes are crimes, since soldiers, farmers, doctors and legacy-hunters all profit from others' harm. He urges Liberalis to bear his debts serenely, let the giver choose the time of repayment, and see that owing well is harder than giving.","ideas":["Eager repayment by any means is ingratitude","Rutilius preferred exile to a return through disaster","Most trades secretly wish for others' misfortune","To owe gracefully is harder than to give"],"terms":[]},{"i":1155,"j":1160,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (1 of 5)","title":"Demetrius' few precepts and the wise man's wealth","summary":"The final book gathers what was left over, and Seneca questions the value of subtle puzzles once the rules of conduct are given. He cites the Cynic Demetrius: like a wrestler with a few well-practised holds, one should keep a few precepts at hand rather than know obscure matters such as tides or twins. The mind that has risen above fear of men and gods, greed and death, and treats the world as its home, has what is needful. Nothing is evil but the base and nothing good but the honourable; true pleasure is freedom from disturbance. The wise man possesses everything, unlike Alexander and the Persian kings, whose conquests only fed their wants.","ideas":["A few precepts in daily use outweigh much learning","Useful truths are easy to find","Pleasure of the body is low and fleeting","The wise man owns all, the conqueror lacks all"],"terms":[{"t":"Demetrius the Cynic","d":"Seneca's contemporary and friend, presented as a model of austere wisdom."}]},{"i":1161,"j":1168,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (2 of 5)","title":"Giving to one who owns everything","summary":"An opponent asks how one can give to the wise man if he owns all things, or to a friend if friends share all. Seneca answers that a thing can belong to two in different ways: the king has authority over all, while citizens own their property; a son or slave can give to father or master; tenant and landlord both have claims on a house, and Cicero's books belong both to their author and to Dorus the bookseller. He dismisses quibbles that the wise man would thus be a procurer or could never buy or borrow. Bion's proofs that all or no one is sacrilegious are answered by distinguishing places consecrated from places merely belonging to the gods.","ideas":["Rule over things differs from ownership of them","The same thing can be owned in two senses","Bion's paradoxes of sacrilege","The wise man owns all in mind, little by law"],"terms":[{"t":"dominium and usus (ownership and use)","d":"The two kinds of claim that let landlord and tenant both call a house theirs."}]},{"i":1169,"j":1174,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (3 of 5)","title":"Demetrius refuses wealth; friends' common goods","summary":"Seneca praises Demetrius as a model sent to his age and imagines him refusing all the world's wealth if he could not give it away. In Demetrius' voice he reviews luxury's trophies, tortoiseshell, knotted tables, crystal, murrhine cups, earrings of pearls, transparent silks, and then gold, silver, and the phantom wealth of bonds and interest, dug up with the iron used for murder. Demetrius laughed off Gaius Caesar's offer of two hundred thousand sesterces. Seneca then shows that friends hold goods in common as parents share both children, or as knights share the theatre seats, so one can still give to a friend. A brief, apparently damaged passage adds that a benefit cannot be more than a benefit.","ideas":["Luxury goods valued above gold","Credit and usury as ghosts of avarice","Demetrius mocked Gaius' small bribe","Common ownership among friends allows giving"],"terms":[]},{"i":1175,"j":1183,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (4 of 5)","title":"Repaying by effort; benefactors gone bad","summary":"Seneca asks whether one who has done everything possible to repay has repaid. Unlike a debtor, a grateful friend is like the physician or advocate who has done his duty though he failed; one who sailed through pirates to ransom a friend already freed has repaid, as Harmodius and Aristogiton and Mucius are honoured for their attempts. His verdict is twofold: the benefactor should count the debt paid, while the receiver should still hold himself bound, so the ungrateful find no excuses. He adds that a benefit from a wise man who later turns bad must still be returned, for folly is a sickness of the mind, and distinguishes the perfect benefit among the wise from the ordinary one, which must be repaid even to a criminal.","ideas":["Ardent effort can count as repayment","Giver should release, receiver should still owe","Gratitude alone is how we repay the gods","Ordinary benefits are repaid even to bad men"],"terms":[]},{"i":1184,"j":1195,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (5 of 5)","title":"Repaying tyrants; forgetting and reminding","summary":"A perfect benefit may be returned to a fallen wise man even if he cannot use it, as one pays a creditor who will waste the money. Seneca doubts a wise man can sink to the worst, but if a benefactor becomes a tyrant like Apollodorus or Phalaris, the bond is broken; he would return only harmless things such as luxuries or saving his child, never arms or money for guards. A Pythagorean who found his shoemaker dead pushed the coins through the door, rebuking his own pleasure at the gain. The rule that the giver should forget his gift is a deliberate exaggeration meaning he should not boast; likewise one may remind modestly, as Socrates did when he said he would have bought a cloak if he had the money.","ideas":["Duty to mankind outweighs debt to a tyrant","The Pythagorean and the dead shoemaker","Stoic precepts overstate to secure the mean","Socrates' gentle hint about the cloak"],"terms":[]},{"i":1196,"j":1210,"loc":"On Benefits, Book VII (end)","title":"Bearing the ungrateful like the gods","summary":"Echoing Aristippus on perfume, Seneca blames grasping creditors for discrediting the honest practice of reminding friends, and contrasts a reproachful quotation from Virgil with a gentle one. Asked how to bear an ingrate who feigns forgetfulness, he answers calmly and generously: ingratitude is expected in a world of insatiable passions, pictured as a city sacked, and each of us has forgotten benefits too. Reproach makes a friend shameless and an enemy; a well-given benefit is like a consecrated offering, never lost. The work ends urging imitation of the gods, who keep giving to those who deny them, and declaring that a great mind loses its bounty and still gives.","ideas":["Remind, never reproach","Ingratitude is a universal failing we share","Gentleness may recover a friend","Imitate the gods and keep giving"],"terms":[]},{"i":1211,"j":1213,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — Serenus","title":"Serenus describes his wavering state of mind","summary":"Serenus writes to Seneca as to a physician, confessing that he is neither free of his vices nor wholly in bondage to them: neither ill nor well. He loves plain furniture, food and servants, yet the luxury of others dazzles him and leaves him doubting his own way of life. He swings between Stoic public service and a retreat into private leisure, and between a plain style of writing and lofty, ambitious speech. He warns that self-flattery keeps many from wisdom, and asks for a remedy, likening his trouble to sea-sickness rather than a storm.","ideas":["A mind halting between good and evil, troubled but not in danger","Attraction to luxury that disturbs but does not change his principles","Oscillation between public life and retirement","We are our own greatest flatterers"],"terms":[{"t":"Zeno, Cleanthes, Chrysippus","d":"Stoic founders who, Serenus notes, urged political participation though none of them took part in it."}]},{"i":1214,"j":1217,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — Seneca's diagnosis","title":"Seneca names the disease: restless self-dissatisfaction","summary":"Seneca compares Serenus to a convalescent who keeps suspecting a fever that has gone; what he needs is confidence that he is on the right path. He names the goal euthymia, after Democritus, and renders it as peace of mind: a steady course in which the mind is pleased with itself, neither elated nor depressed. He then lays out the wider disease, whose single effect is dissatisfaction with oneself, arising from frustrated or unspoken desires, idleness, envy and fickleness. Its sufferers travel restlessly from place to place, but, as Lucretius' line suggests, no one escapes himself; some are driven to sickness of life itself.","ideas":["Peace of mind as a steady, self-content condition of the mind","Frustrated desires breed vacillation, gloom, envy and boredom","Restless travel cannot cure a fault that lies in ourselves","Weariness of repetition can drive men to death"],"terms":[{"t":"euthymia (cheerfulness, peace of mind)","d":"The Greek term, from Democritus' treatise, which Seneca translates as tranquillity or peace of mind."}]},{"i":1218,"j":1220,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — public life and retirement","title":"Athenodorus on retirement, and Seneca's reply","summary":"Seneca reports Athenodorus' advice: the best cure for ennui is public business, but since innocence is unsafe in politics a man may withdraw and still serve mankind by teaching virtue, like a soldier guarding the gates. Seneca thinks Athenodorus retreated too quickly: one should withdraw slowly, without abandoning one's post, finding whatever form of service remains open, down to being a good friend or a citizen of the world. Virtue does good even when hidden, as remedies help by their smell. Socrates moving freely among the Thirty Tyrants shows that a wise man can act even in an oppressed state, though one may sometimes put into harbour and give more time to leisure and letters.","ideas":["Philosophy in private can be a form of public service","Retire gradually, keeping one's honour like a soldier","If one sphere of action closes, others remain open","Socrates free amid thirty masters"],"terms":[{"t":"Athenodorus","d":"Stoic philosopher whose views on public business and withdrawal Seneca quotes at length and partly rejects."}]},{"i":1221,"j":1224,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — self, tasks, friends, property","title":"Judging oneself, one's tasks, friends and wealth","summary":"Seneca says one must first assess oneself, then the business undertaken, then the people involved. Know your own limits of temper and strength, take on only tasks you can finish or leave, and choose your career by natural bent, as Isocrates steered Ephorus to history. Friends should be as free from vice as possible, since vices are contagious, and grumblers ruin one's peace even if loyal. He turns to property as the chief source of sorrow: losing money hurts more than never having it, and Diogenes, who let his runaway slave go, is happy because he cannot be robbed; the best fortune is one close to poverty.","ideas":["Examine self, business and companions before acting","Avoid tasks that grow beyond one's control","Friendship as a great joy, but vice spreads like plague","Diogenes and the gods possess nothing and lose nothing"],"terms":[{"t":"Diogenes","d":"The Cynic, praised for making it impossible that anything should be taken from him."}]},{"i":1225,"j":1227,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — thrift, endurance, the wise man","title":"Thrift, bearing one's chains, and returning fortune's loans","summary":"Seneca urges thrift and measuring things by use rather than show, drawing in one's sails against Fortune; even book collecting is criticised, and the Alexandrian library called learned luxury. If caught in a snare one cannot escape, habit makes chains easy to bear: all men are chained to Fortune, some with gold, and all life is slavery, so one should seek the good within reach and set limits to one's own successes. The wise man needs none of these cautions: he holds body and self as borrowed, and will return them gladly to Fortune and Nature. Fearing death makes one live badly, and Seneca closes with Publilius' line that what befalls one may befall all.","ideas":["Measure possessions by use, not ornament; books are for study, not display","Habit softens misfortune; everyone wears some chain","The wise man treats all he has as a trust to be returned","One cannot live well without knowing how to die well"],"terms":[{"t":"Publilius","d":"Roman writer of mimes whom Seneca quotes for a noble line despite his low genre."}]},{"i":1228,"j":1232,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — foresight and steadiness","title":"Expecting misfortune; purposeful action; Julius Kanus","summary":"Seneca argues that whoever expects others' misfortunes as possible for himself is armed beforehand, citing the falls of Pompeius, Sejanus, Croesus and others. One should neither labour for vain things nor labour in vain, and he mocks the aimless busybodies who rush about the city like ants. Following Democritus, one should avoid unnecessary business and add a reservation to every plan, so that nothing befalls the wise man unexpected. One should be adaptable without caprice and turn the mind inward, as shown by Zeno after his shipwreck, Theodorus, and above all Julius Kanus, who met Gaius' death sentence calmly and resolved to observe his own soul leaving the body.","ideas":["Foreseen evils lose their sting","Restless indolence: busyness without purpose","The reservation clause: 'unless something hinders'","Kanus learning even from his own death"],"terms":[{"t":"Julius Kanus","d":"A victim of the emperor Gaius whose serenity before execution Seneca presents as the philosophic life carried furthest."}]},{"i":1233,"j":1236,"loc":"On Tranquillity of Mind — misanthropy, masks, relaxation","title":"Laughing at vice, honest living, and rest","summary":"Against despair at human wickedness, Seneca prefers Democritus' laughter to Heraclitus' tears, though best of all is calm acceptance; grief should follow reason, not custom. The deaths of good men such as Socrates, Cato and Regulus should inspire rather than grieve, since they won immortality. Living behind a mask is a torment, so one should be open, alternate solitude and company, and give the mind rest, as Socrates, Cato, Scipio and Asinius Pollio did; even wine in moderation frees the mind, and inspiration needs a touch of madness. He closes by telling Serenus that none of these remedies suffices without constant watch over the wavering mind.","ideas":["Democritus laughing versus Heraclitus weeping","The brave deaths of good men are not to be mourned","Hypocrisy is exhausting; simplicity is not slovenliness","The mind needs alternation of work and play"],"terms":[{"t":"Liber","d":"The god of wine, whose name Seneca links to his freeing the mind from cares."}]},{"i":1237,"j":1240,"loc":"On Providence — introduction and opening argument","title":"Why do evils befall good men?","summary":"An editorial introduction outlines the dialogue: Lucilius asks how misfortunes to good men fit with providence, and Seneca answers on Stoic lines. Seneca sets aside a full proof that providence governs the ordered cosmos, briefly pointing to the stars, tides and even storms as following law. He argues that the good man is kin to the gods, and God, like a strict father, trains and hardens him; no evil can befall a good man, since contraries cannot combine, and adversity is a trial of firmness, as athletes seek strong opponents. The gods find no nobler spectacle than a brave man matched with evil fortune, such as Cato resolving on death amid the ruin of the republic.","ideas":["Cosmic order shows providence","God loves good men as a stern father, not an indulgent mother","What matters is how one bears things, not what one bears","Cato against fortune as a sight worthy of the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"providence","d":"The divine governance of the universe, whose justice toward good men the dialogue defends."}]},{"i":1241,"j":1244,"loc":"On Providence — misfortunes benefit the sufferer","title":"Misfortune as the testing of the noble","summary":"Seneca imagines the gods watching Cato's death, his first wound failing so his courage could act a second time. He sets out his programme: apparent evils benefit those who suffer them and all mankind, come by destiny and the same law that makes men good, and the good man should never be pitied. Like surgery, misfortunes cure; Demetrius held no one more unhappy than the man never tried. Fortune seeks the bravest opponents, as with Mucius, Fabricius, Rutilius, Regulus, Socrates and Cato, who are contrasted with the pampered Sulla and Maecenas; Nature herself chose Cato to show her terrors are no real evils.","ideas":["Seneca's plan of argument for the dialogue","Hardship is like a painful but healing remedy","Fortune scorns cowards and picks worthy opponents","Regulus on the rack is happier than sleepless Maecenas"],"terms":[{"t":"Demetrius","d":"Cynic philosopher, Seneca's contemporary, whose sayings he quotes on the unhappiness of the untried."}]},{"i":1245,"j":1246,"loc":"On Providence — virtue's opportunity","title":"Without trial no one knows his strength","summary":"Seneca argues that constant prosperity leaves a man ignorant of half of nature and of himself; misfortune is virtue's opportunity, as storms show the pilot. God hardens those he loves, as generals send their best troops on dangerous missions and Spartan fathers have their sons flogged, while luxury softens and endangers. Hardship becomes nature through use, as with the Germans and Danube tribes, and trees grow strong by being shaken. He adds that it benefits all that good men labour, since God thereby shows that riches and the like are neither good nor bad, and quotes Demetrius offering the gods willingly whatever they ask.","ideas":["Misfortune reveals and strengthens virtue","Excess of comfort is the most harmful excess","Goods given to the worst men show they are not goods","Willing surrender cannot be robbed"],"terms":[]},{"i":1247,"j":1266,"loc":"On Providence — fate and the open door","title":"Following fate; Phaethon; God's own defence","summary":"Seneca declares himself not God's slave but his willing follower, since a single chain of fate governs men and gods alike; the creator obeys the fates he once decreed. The workman cannot alter his materials, and strong natures must face rough roads, as fire tries gold. The Sun's warnings to Phaethon about the steep path through the sky, and Phaethon's eager reply, show that courage loves a lofty path. Finally God is imagined speaking: he has given good men inner goods instead of hollow show, strength to bear hardship, and made death easy and always near so that no one is held captive against his will.","ideas":["One unchangeable stream of destiny bears men and gods","Hard paths belong to strong natures","Good men's goods are within, not on the surface","Death as the open way of escape"],"terms":[{"t":"fatum (fate)","d":"The everlasting chain of causes that decides all things, to which the good man submits willingly."}]}]});